OAI_PAPER({"slug": "No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026", "title": "No bigeodesics in planar first-passage percolation", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/main.pdf", "family": "212", "family_title": "Planar first-passage geometry and the absence of bigeodesics", "abstract_html": "We prove that planar first-passage percolation with independent identically distributed nonnegative nonatomic edge weights has almost surely no doubly infinite geodesic, provided the minimum of four independent weights has finite second moment. This resolves the planar no-bigeodesics conjecture under that moment assumption. The conclusion rules out all bigeodesics simultaneously, without any regularity assumption on the limit shape.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal E\\) be the set of unoriented nearest-neighbor edges of \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal E\\) be the set of unoriented nearest-neighbor edges of \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\). Give the edges independent weights \\(t_e\\) with common nonatomic Borel probability law \\(G\\) on \\([0,\\infty)\\). For \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\), let \\(T(x,y)\\) be the infimum of the costs of finite nearest-neighbor walks from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\), with every edge traversal charged its weight.</p><p>Assume that, for independent \\(X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4\\) with law \\(G\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\min\\{X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4\\}^{\\,2}\\right]&lt;\\infty. \\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>Then, with probability one, there is no sequence \\((v_i)_{i\\in\\mathbb Z}\\) of pairwise distinct vertices, with consecutive vertices adjacent, such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=i}^{j-1}t_{\\{v_k,v_{k+1}\\}}=T(v_i,v_j)\n\\qquad\\text{for every }i&lt;j.\n\\]</p><p>Such a sequence is called a bigeodesic. The conclusion excludes all bigeodesics simultaneously; neither end label nor end direction is prescribed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose bigeodesics exist with positive probability. Every bigeodesic contains a lattice vertex. Countability of vertices and charts in Proposition 4, together with deterministic-label exclusions, gives a chart in which a bigeodesic through some fixed vertex has positive probability. Translate that vertex to zero and use the chart coordinates.</p><p>Proposition 4 confines its arms in \\(Q^\\pm(0,H)\\) for some finite \\(H\\), almost surely. Countable union over integer \\(H\\) gives deterministic \\(H_0\\ge1\\) for which \\(\\mathcal B(0)\\) in Proposition 14 has probability \\(p&gt;0\\). Removing the countably many partition endpoints does not change the probability, by Proposition 3.</p><p>Apply Proposition 14 for every positive integer \\(M\\), using the fixed spacing \\(l\\) from Proposition 15. Impose two filters.</p><p>First, for horizontal \\(k\\), let \\(\\mathcal R_K(k)\\) be the base-environment event that every ray from \\(k\\) with label in \\(I\\) lies in \\(Q^+(k,K)\\), and every ray with label in \\(-I\\) lies in \\(Q^-(k,K)\\). Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\mu(\\mathcal R_K(k)^c)\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(K\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nChoose one deterministic \\(K\\ge1\\), independent of \\(M\\), with failure probability at most \\(p/8\\). Translation invariance makes it the same at every horizontal site. Retain\n\\[\n\\widetilde J_M(k)=J_M(k)\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal R_K(k)}.\n\\]\nWithout any independence assertion,\n\\[\nq_M:=\\mathbb P(\\widetilde J_M(k)=1)\n\\ge\\mathbb P(J_M(k)=1)-\\mu(\\mathcal R_K(k)^c)\n\\ge p/8. \\tag{80}\n\\]\nThis environment-only regularity event is universally measurable by Proposition 1 and remains a separate filter outside existential path projections. The translated rule preserves equal marginals.</p><p>The near radius \\(R_0\\), spacing \\(l\\), and comparator offset \\(K\\) are now fixed independently of \\(M\\). Fix \\(M\\). Its \\(R(M)\\) is finite. Selected arms have offset \\(H_0\\); base comparators have offset \\(K\\); the changed region has radius at most \\(D_{R(M)}\\); and the gain portion has radius at most \\(R(M)\\). Proposition 8 therefore supplies a finite deterministic separation \\(S(M)\\), uniform over every bin of every \\(\\Pi_j\\), for comparison with gain \\(b=M\\). Its growth with \\(M\\) does not change \\(l\\).</p><p>For partition \\(\\Pi_j\\), retain a site with \\(\\widetilde J_M(k)=1\\) only if there is no other retained site \\(y\\) such that\n\\[\n0&lt;|y-k|\\le S(M)\n\\]\nand \\(\\rho_y,\\rho_k\\) lie in the same bin. Write \\(J_{M,j}(k)\\) for this final indicator and \\(q_{M,j}\\) for its common marginal. The test uses at most\n\\[\n2\\lfloor S(M)/l\\rfloor\n\\]\nneighbors. Two surviving sites in one bin are separated by more than \\(S(M)\\), so Proposition 8 applies to consecutive surviving marks.</p><p>For fixed \\(M,k\\), this second filter disappears as \\(j\\to\\infty\\). On the common event of Proposition 15, every other marked site has label different from \\(\\rho_k\\). The nested partitions eventually separate \\(\\rho_k\\) from each of the finitely many retained neighbors within distance \\(S(M)\\). Taking the maximum of their finitely many separating indices shows that \\(k\\) survives for every later partition. If \\(\\widetilde J_M(k)=0\\), all final indicators are zero. Hence\n\\[\nJ_{M,j}(k)\\longrightarrow\\widetilde J_M(k)\n\\quad\\text{almost surely},\n\\qquad\nq_{M,j}\\longrightarrow q_M \\tag{81}\n\\]\nby bounded convergence. No rate of random-label separation is required.</p><p>For a bin \\((\\alpha,\\beta)_I\\) of \\(\\Pi_j\\), let \\(q_{M,j}^{\\alpha,\\beta}\\) be the probability that a site survives with its label in that bin. Proposition 9 with \\(b=M\\) gives\n\\[\nq_{M,j}^{\\alpha,\\beta}\n\\le\\frac lM\\bigl(r(\\beta)-r(\\alpha)\\bigr).\n\\]\nEvery selected label avoids partition endpoints, so every surviving mark belongs to exactly one bin. Summing the finite partition widths gives\n\\[\nq_{M,j}\\le\\frac{lW_I}{M}. \\tag{82}\n\\]\nThe label budget permits all dependencies introduced by shared raises and both filters.</p><p>Send \\(j\\to\\infty\\) for this fixed \\(M\\). Equations (80)–(82) imply\n\\[\n\\frac p8\\le q_M\\le\\frac{lW_I}{M}.\n\\]\nThe quantities \\(p,l,W_I\\) were fixed before choosing \\(M\\). Any integer\n\\[\nM&gt;\\frac{8lW_I}{p}\n\\]\nis a contradiction.</p><p>The reduction used countably many vertices and charts. The excluded deterministic labels, integer radii, sites, and union metrics form countable collections. Equality of Busemann pairs and distinctness of marked labels hold pathwise for environment-dependent labels. The contradiction therefore excludes the existence event itself and proves almost-sure absence of all bigeodesics.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Basic metric, Busemann, finite-modification, and measurability properties.", "statement": "<p>**Basic metric, Busemann, finite-modification, and measurability properties. Sources: Lemmas 2.1, 2.2, 2.6, and 2.7.**</p><p>Use the weight law and probability space\n\\[\n\\Omega=[0,\\infty)^{\\mathcal E},\n\\qquad \\mathbb P=G^{\\mathcal E}\n\\]\nfrom Theorem 1, initially with the product Borel sigma-algebra and subsequently with its completion.</p><p>A finite geodesic realizes \\(T(x,y)\\). A ray \\(p=(p_0,p_1,\\ldots)\\) is a simple infinite nearest-neighbor path every finite segment of which is a geodesic. Two rays coalesce if their vertex sequences agree after deleting finite initial portions.</p><p>The following properties hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Almost surely every pair of vertices has a unique finite geodesic.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every ray \\(p\\), the Busemann function\n   \\[\n   B_p(x,y)=\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\bigl(T(x,p_j)-T(y,p_j)\\bigr) \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   exists for all vertices \\(x,y\\), is additive and antisymmetric, satisfies\n   \\[\n   |B_p(x,y)|\\le T(x,y),\n   \\qquad\n   B_p(p_i,p_j)=T(p_i,p_j)\\quad(i\\le j),\n   \\]\n   and obeys\n   \\[\n   0\\le T(x,p_j)-B_p(x,p_j)\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Deleting a finite initial portion does not change \\(B_p\\); coalescing rays have the same Busemann function.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If two environments differ on a finite edge set \\(F\\), then, with\n   \\[\n   K=\\sum_{e\\in F}|t'_e-t_e|,\n   \\]\n   their passage distances satisfy\n   \\[\n   |T'(x,y)-T(x,y)|\\le K.\n   \\]\n   A ray retained in both environments, with asymptotically linear Busemann functions in both, has the same linear functional in both. The same applies to a retained common tail. Under an increase of edge weights, a geodesic using only unchanged edges remains geodesic.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The path conditions used below—being a ray or bigeodesic, coalescence, and asymptotic linearity to a specified functional—are Borel conditions on environment, path, and functional spaces. Their existence projections, with additional Borel tests, are analytic and universally measurable. Witnesses can be selected completion-measurably. Counts of distinct labels admitting witnesses are measurable in the completed probability space, with value \\(+\\infty\\) allowed.</p><p>An environment with law absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\mathbb P\\) inherits each specified \\(\\mathbb P\\)-almost-sure assertion. Common events may therefore be taken for every countable collection of such environments and deterministic parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.4 establish the asserted metric, Busemann, finite-change, and measurability properties. Absolute continuity transfers specified full-measure events, and countable intersection gives their simultaneous validity for the countable collections of environments and deterministic parameters used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Finite minimizers.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition 1, almost surely every pair of vertices has a unique finite geodesic.", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 1, almost surely every pair of vertices has a unique finite geodesic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every edge weight is strictly positive almost surely. By dominated convergence, choose \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\na=4\\mathbb E e^{-ct_e}&lt;1.\n\\]\nFor a fixed vertex \\(x\\) and fixed \\(K&lt;\\infty\\), the probability that some simple path of \\(j\\) edges from \\(x\\) has cost at most \\(K\\) is at most\n\\[\ne^{cK}a^j.\n\\]\nThe sum over \\(j\\) is finite. Consequently only finitely many simple paths from \\(x\\) have cost at most \\(K\\), almost surely. Take the countable intersection over vertices and positive integer \\(K\\).</p><p>Every finite walk can be shortened to a simple path by erasing loops. Comparing with any fixed path between two vertices shows that the infimum is attained among a finite collection of paths.</p><p>Two different simple paths with the same endpoints have an edge in their symmetric difference. Conditional on all other weights, equality of their costs prescribes one value of the independent nonatomic weight on that edge and thus has probability zero. There are only countably many pairs of finite paths.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, “Elementary Busemann properties.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In an environment with finite geodesics, let \\(p\\) be a ray.", "statement": "<p>In an environment with finite geodesics, let \\(p\\) be a ray. Then the limit (2) exists for all vertices \\(x,y\\), is additive and antisymmetric, satisfies \\(|B_p(x,y)|\\le T(x,y)\\), and is calibrated on the ray:\n\\[\nB_p(p_i,p_j)=T(p_i,p_j)\\qquad(i\\le j).\n\\]\nDeleting a finite initial portion does not change the Busemann function. Coalescing rays have the same Busemann function. For each fixed vertex \\(x\\), (3) holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>The sequence\n\\[\na_j(x)=T(x,p_j)-T(p_0,p_j)\n\\]\nis decreasing, by the triangle inequality and calibration of the segments of \\(p\\), and is bounded below by \\(-T(p_0,x)\\). Write \\(a_\\infty(x)\\) for its limit. Then\n\\[\nB_p(x,y)=a_\\infty(x)-a_\\infty(y).\n\\]\nAdditivity, antisymmetry, and the metric bound follow immediately.</p><p>For \\(j\\ge i\\) and \\(k\\ge j\\), the geodesic property gives\n\\[\nT(p_i,p_k)-T(p_j,p_k)=T(p_i,p_j),\n\\]\nproving calibration. Tail invariance and the coalescence assertion follow from the defining limit. Finally,\n\\[\nB_p(x,p_j)\n=B_p(x,p_0)+T(p_0,p_j)\n=a_\\infty(x)+T(p_0,p_j),\n\\]\nso the left side of (3) is \\(a_j(x)-a_\\infty(x)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.6, “Finite modifications.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For this deterministic comparison, let two environments differ on a finite edge set \\(F\\), and put \\( K=\\sum_{e\\in F}|t'_e-t_e|. \\) Then \\( |T'(x,y)-T(x,y)|\\le K \\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2). \\) If a path is a ray in both …", "statement": "<p>For this deterministic comparison, let two environments differ on a finite edge set \\(F\\), and put\n\\[\nK=\\sum_{e\\in F}|t'_e-t_e|.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|T'(x,y)-T(x,y)|\\le K\n\\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2).\n\\]</p><p>If a path is a ray in both environments and has asymptotically linear Busemann functions in both, its linear functional is the same in the two environments. This also applies when a common tail is a ray in both. If \\(t'_e\\ge t_e\\) for all edges and a geodesic path uses only unchanged edges, it remains geodesic in the new metric.</p>", "proof": "<p>Loop erasure allows the passage-time infimum to be taken over simple paths. Each such path uses every changed edge at most once, so its cost changes by at most \\(K\\). Taking infima gives the distance bound.</p><p>For a retained ray \\(p\\), passing to its defining Busemann limits gives\n\\[\n|B'_p(x,y)-B_p(x,y)|\\le2K.\n\\]\nLinear asymptotics along \\(x=nv\\), for two independent lattice vectors \\(v\\), imply equality of the linear parts. Tail invariance handles the stated extension.</p><p>Under an increase, every competitor is at least as expensive as before, whereas each segment of the retained path has unchanged cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.7, “Path witnesses and selection,” and the conventions following it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The conditions that a specified path is a ray or bigeodesic, that specified rays coalesce, and that a ray’s Busemann function is asymptotically linear to a specified functional are Borel conditions on the corresponding …", "statement": "<p>The conditions that a specified path is a ray or bigeodesic, that specified rays coalesce, and that a ray’s Busemann function is asymptotically linear to a specified functional are Borel conditions on the corresponding environment, path, and functional spaces.</p><p>Existence events obtained by projecting these conditions, with additional Borel tests, are analytic and universally measurable. On such events a witnessing path can be selected completion-measurably. The number of distinct labels with such witnesses is measurable in the completed probability space, allowing \\(+\\infty\\).</p><p>The failure of an environment-only condition requiring all rays from specified vertices, with labels in a fixed chart, to satisfy a Borel regularity test has an analytic violating-ray witness. Such a regularity event is coanalytic and universally measurable; it is imposed separately from existential path projections.</p>", "proof": "<p>The spaces\n\\[\n\\Omega,\\qquad(\\mathbb Z^2)^{\\mathbb N},\n\\qquad(\\mathbb Z^2)^{\\mathbb Z}\n\\]\nare standard Borel spaces. Nearest-neighbor adjacency and simplicity are countable coordinate tests. Each finite segment minimizes exactly when its cost is no greater than that of every finite competitor with the same endpoints; there are countably many competitors. Equivalently, passage distance is a countable infimum of Borel finite-path costs.</p><p>Limits defining Busemann values are countable tests, as are asymptotic linearity with rational tolerances and integer radii. Coalescence is expressed by\n\\[\n\\exists i,j\\ge0\\ \\forall k\\ge0:\\ p_{i+k}=q_{j+k}.\n\\]\nThe analytic projection and Jankov–von Neumann selection theorems give the existence and selection assertions [Kec95, Theorems 29.7 and 29.9]. To test that a label count is at least \\(j\\), project the Borel condition for \\(j\\) path witnesses with pairwise distinct labels.</p><p>A universal ray-regularity condition fails exactly when a violating ray exists, giving the stated coanalytic event. When combined with an existential path condition, first project the Borel witness relation and then intersect with this separate regularity event.</p><p>For a fixed law on a standard Borel space, a completion-measurable map into a standard Borel space has a Borel version. Thus conditioning on an output environment fixes any path selected from that environment, up to a null set. Translated constructions use the same selector on the relatively shifted input, so their one-site marginal laws agree.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition A.3, “The fixed-functional boundary comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assume the iid nonatomic setting and minimum-of-four second-moment condition of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>Assume the iid nonatomic setting and minimum-of-four second-moment condition of Theorem 1.</p><p>A random coalescing geodesic is a measurable choice of a ray from the origin whose lattice translates coalesce almost surely [AH19, Definition 2.3]. Write \\(\\mathcal G(x)\\) for the translated ray from \\(x\\), and \\(B_{\\mathcal G}\\) for their common full-plane Busemann function.</p><p>Then two random coalescing geodesics with the same full-plane Busemann linear functional agree almost surely.</p><p>The proof uses the full-plane linearity established in [AH19, Proposition 4.2], which precedes the boundary argument. It replaces the boundary-expectation step in [AH19, Proposition 4.4] without assuming integrability of restricted passage times or restricted boundary Busemann increments.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let the two families be \\(\\mathcal G,\\mathcal G'\\), with common functional \\(\\rho\\). By [AH19, Proposition 4.3], their contact sector is contained in the interior of one of the eight half-planes \\(H\\). Both families eventually enter every translate of \\(H\\).</p><p>Recall first the geometric part of [AH19, Lemma 4.6, Section 4.4], which does not use its subsequent expectation assertion. There is positive probability that the ray from the origin stays in \\(H\\): on an eventual-entrance ray, a vertex minimizing the normal coordinate starts a tail contained in its translated half-plane. Finite uniqueness and coalescence identify this suffix with the selected ray from that vertex. Countability of the vertices and covariance give positive probability at a fixed vertex. Boundary translations of an iid environment are ergodic, also for diagonal boundaries. Hence there are such starting vertices in both directions along the boundary.</p><p>Choose two such roots on opposite sides of the origin. Their contained rays coalesce, and their portions up to their first common vertex form a finite simple connecting arc. If the origin lies on this arc, its selected suffix is already contained in \\(H\\). Otherwise, choose consecutive boundary contacts of the arc on opposite sides of the origin. The intervening subarc, together with the boundary segment, encloses a bounded region adjacent to the origin.</p><p>Choose \\(w\\) on the common tail of the two bounding rays and the target ray, outside this region and beyond the connecting arc. Every restricted minimizer from the origin to a target beyond \\(w\\) must meet the separating subarc. From that meeting point the bounding-ray suffix to the target lies in \\(H\\) and is a full-plane minimizer. Restricted uniqueness forces this suffix, hence passage through \\(w\\). The prefix to \\(w\\) is the fixed restricted minimizer to \\(w\\). This proves stabilization and coalescence with the full-plane family, including when the bounding rays revisit the boundary.</p><p>Applying the construction at every boundary root gives restricted families \\(\\mathcal G_H,\\mathcal G'_H\\); their boundary Busemann functions are exactly the restricted limits in Lemma 2.1.</p><p>Suppose the families differ with positive probability. Since each restricted family coalesces, the event that the two families coalesce is invariant under boundary translations. If they coalesce, finite uniqueness makes their rays from any common root identical. Boundary ergodicity therefore makes distinctness an almost-sure event. The same invariance and ergodicity make their asymptotic planar order deterministic, as in [AH19, Section 4.5]. Orient \\(z\\) so that the finite crossing comparison in equation (12) of that source gives\n\\[\n\\Delta(k,l)\n:=B_{\\mathcal G}^{H}(kz,lz)-B_{\\mathcal G'}^{H}(kz,lz)\n\\ge0,\\qquad k&lt;l. \\tag{84}\n\\]</p><p>The comparison chooses a crossing of \\(\\mathcal G_H(kz)\\) with \\(\\mathcal G'_H(lz)\\), and common-tail vertices \\(x\\) of the primed rays and \\(y\\) of the unprimed rays, both beyond the crossing. Swapping the two pieces at the crossing gives walks \\(\\pi_x\\) from \\(kz\\) to \\(x\\) and \\(\\pi_y\\) from \\(lz\\) to \\(y\\), with\n\\[\n\\Delta(k,l)\n=\\bigl(T_H(\\pi_y)-T_H(lz,y)\\bigr)\n +\\bigl(T_H(\\pi_x)-T_H(kz,x)\\bigr).\n\\]\nBoth summands are nonnegative finite excess costs. This identity is purely geometric.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nZ(x)=D_{\\mathcal G}(x)-D_{\\mathcal G'}(x).\n\\]\nEquation (83) gives\n\\[\n\\Delta(0,n)\n=B_{\\mathcal G}(0,nz)-B_{\\mathcal G'}(0,nz)+Z(0)-Z(nz).\n\\]\nThe first difference divided by \\(n\\) tends to zero almost surely, by full-plane linearity and equality of the functionals. Finiteness and stationarity give, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(|Z(nz)|&gt;n\\varepsilon)\n=\\mathbb P(|Z(0)|&gt;n\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\Delta(0,n)/n\\to0\\) in probability. Lemma 2.2 and (84) imply\n\\[\n\\Delta(k,l)=0\n\\quad\\text{for every pair, almost surely}.\n\\]</p><p>The final uniqueness argument is the finite one in [AH19, Section 4.5]. Distinct same-root restricted rays have a finite common initial portion. Choose a deterministic bound \\(m\\) on its length holding with positive probability; boundary ergodicity gives two such roots farther apart than \\(2m\\). In the crossing comparison, at least one swapped walk differs from the corresponding minimizing path. Indeed, if both swapped walks were their unique geodesics, the crossing vertex would lie in the common initial portion from each root, making the lattice distance between the roots at most \\(2m\\).</p><p>Since both nonnegative excesses sum to zero, a distinct swapped walk is also minimizing, contrary to uniqueness. Thus the restricted families agree. Their coalescence with the full-plane families and finite uniqueness imply \\(\\mathcal G=\\mathcal G'\\).</p><p>This supplies [AH19, Proposition 4.4] without the boundary-expectation step. The other later direct use of their Lemma 4.6, in the proof of their Lemma 9.6, uses only the geometric construction and coalescence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma A.1, “Finite boundary correction.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be a random coalescing geodesic as in Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal G\\) be a random coalescing geodesic as in Proposition 2. Let \\(H\\) be one of the eight axial or diagonal half-planes used in [AH19], let \\(z\\) be a primitive lattice vector parallel to its boundary, and let \\(T_H\\) be passage distance restricted to \\(H\\). Assume that \\(\\mathcal G\\) eventually enters every translate of \\(H\\).</p><p>For \\(x\\in H\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), the limit\n\\[\nD_{\\mathcal G}(x)\n=\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\bigl(T_H(x,v_j)-T(x,v_j)\\bigr)\n\\]\nexists and is finite and nonnegative, where \\((v_j)\\) is any ray of \\(\\mathcal G\\) and the targets are sufficiently far along its \\(H\\)-contained tail. The limit is independent of the target ray.</p><p>The variables \\(D_{\\mathcal G}(kz)\\), \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\), are stationary under boundary translations, and the restricted Busemann limit satisfies\n\\[\nB_{\\mathcal G}^{H}(x,y)\n=B_{\\mathcal G}(x,y)+D_{\\mathcal G}(x)-D_{\\mathcal G}(y). \\tag{83}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite restricted minimizers exist and are unique: restricted simple paths form a subcollection of full-plane simple paths, so the finite-path argument of Proposition 1 applies, as does the nonatomic tie argument. The half-plane lattice is connected and all edge weights are finite.</p><p>Choose a vertex \\(a\\) after the last exit of the target ray from \\(H\\). Its segment to each later \\(v_j\\) minimizes for both metrics, so\n\\[\nT_H(a,v_j)=T(a,v_j).\n\\]\nEach of\n\\[\nT_H(x,v_j)-T(a,v_j),\n\\qquad\nT(x,v_j)-T(a,v_j)\n\\]\nis decreasing by the triangle inequality along this calibrated tail. They are bounded in absolute value by \\(T_H(x,a)\\) and \\(T(x,a)\\), respectively. Their limits therefore exist and are finite. Their difference is nonnegative, since \\(T_H\\ge T\\), and defines \\(D_{\\mathcal G}(x)\\).</p><p>Any two rays of the family have a common tail, so changing the target ray leaves the limit unchanged. Translation by \\(kz\\) preserves \\(H\\) and replaces the target ray by another translated ray of the same family. This proves covariance and hence stationarity. No choice of the auxiliary anchor \\(a\\) enters the definition.</p><p>Measurability follows from the measurable ray choice and countable limits defining distances. Subtracting the distance limits at \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) proves (83) and the existence of \\(B_{\\mathcal G}^{H}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma A.2, “A nonnegative stationary difference.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For this lemma only, no percolation assumptions are needed.", "statement": "<p>For this lemma only, no percolation assumptions are needed. Let\n\\[\n\\Delta(k,l),\\qquad k,l\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nbe a finite additive stationary process, and assume\n\\[\n\\Delta(k,k+1)\\ge0\\quad\\text{almost surely}.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta(0,n)}n\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\text{in probability},\n\\]\nthen every increment is zero almost surely.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(X_k=\\min\\{\\Delta(k,k+1),1\\}\\). Then\n\\[\n0\\le A_n:=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}X_k\n\\le\\frac{\\Delta(0,n)}n,\n\\qquad A_n\\le1.\n\\]\nThus \\(A_n\\to0\\) in probability and\n\\[\n\\mathbb EA_n\\le\\varepsilon+\\mathbb P(A_n&gt;\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow\\varepsilon\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Stationarity gives \\(\\mathbb EA_n=\\mathbb EX_0\\) for all \\(n\\), so \\(X_0=0\\) almost surely. Stationarity, countability, and additivity prove the assertion for every pair. Neither integrability of \\(\\Delta\\) nor ergodicity is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Structural input and its consequences.", "statement": "<p>**Structural input and its consequences. Sources: Theorem 2.3, Corollary 2.4, and Lemma 2.5.**</p><p>Assume the setting and moment condition of Theorem 1. For a norm \\(m\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^2\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\n=\\partial\\{\\rho\\in(\\mathbb R^2)^*:\\rho(v)\\le m(v)\\text{ for all }v\\in\\mathbb R^2\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA(\\rho)=\\{d\\in S^1:m(d)=\\rho(d)\\}.\n\\]\nHere \\(|\\cdot|\\) is Euclidean norm and \\(S^1\\) the Euclidean unit circle.</p><p>There are a deterministic norm \\(m\\) and deterministic closed set \\(\\mathcal C\\subseteq\\mathcal S\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Almost surely,\n   \\[\n   T(0,x)=m(x)+o(|x|).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Almost surely, simultaneously for all rays \\(p\\) from all lattice vertices, there is a unique \\(\\rho_p\\in\\mathcal C\\) such that\n   \\[\n   B_p(0,x)=\\rho_p(x)+o(|x|)\n   \\qquad(|x|\\to\\infty). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The limiting directions of \\(p\\) lie in \\(A(\\rho_p)\\). At each vertex the set of realized functionals is \\(\\mathcal C\\). Call \\(\\rho_p\\) the label of \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For each fixed \\(\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\), almost surely there is exactly one ray \\(g_\\rho(x)\\) of label \\(\\rho\\) from each \\(x\\). These rays coalesce and are backwards finite: for each \\(z\\), only finitely many \\(x\\) have \\(z\\in g_\\rho(x)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The norm \\(m\\) and set \\(\\mathcal C\\) have the rotation and reflection symmetries of the square lattice.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Almost surely,\n   \\[\n   T(x,y)=m(y-x)+o(|x|+|y|)\n   \\qquad(|x|+|y|\\to\\infty), \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   uniformly over both endpoints.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. For each fixed \\(\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\), almost surely no bigeodesic has an end of label \\(\\rho\\). This exclusion holds simultaneously for every member of any countable deterministic subset of \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(\\rho\\), write \\(B_\\rho\\) for the common Busemann function of its coalescing family. The event for the fixed-label assertion may depend on \\(\\rho\\); no uncountable intersection of these events is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 supplies the structural assertions. Lemma 3.2 supplies the uniform approximation, and Lemma 3.3 supplies the fixed-label bigeodesic exclusion. Fixed-label coalescence and Proposition 1 give the common function \\(B_\\rho\\). The distinction between the simultaneous all-ray statement and the label-dependent fixed-label event is retained throughout.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Theorem 2.3, “Ahlberg–Hoffman and the shape theorem.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the assumptions of Proposition 3, there are \\(m\\) and \\(\\mathcal C\\) satisfying assertions 1–4 of that proposition, with simultaneous all-ray asymptotic linearity as in (4), and fixed-label uniqueness …", "statement": "<p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 3, there are \\(m\\) and \\(\\mathcal C\\) satisfying assertions 1–4 of that proposition, with simultaneous all-ray asymptotic linearity as in (4), and fixed-label uniqueness, coalescence, and backward finiteness as stated there.</p>", "proof": "<p>The source statements are [AH19, Assumption A1, Theorems 2.5–2.6 and 10.8–10.11, and Proposition 4.5]. Assumption A1 is the iid continuous-marginal setting with the second moment of the minimum of four incident edge weights. A nonatomic Borel law has a continuous distribution function, so singular continuous laws are included.</p><p>The iid shape theorem is due to Cox and Durrett [CD81] and is recalled in [AH19, Section 3.1]. Their Theorem 10.8 supplies a random coalescing geodesic for each fixed member of \\(\\mathcal C\\); Theorem 10.11 and its proof identify its translates as the unique rays of that functional; Proposition 4.5 gives backward finiteness. Countability of the lattice permits all starting vertices.</p><p>Proposition 2 replaces the boundary-expectation step used in [AH19, Proposition 4.4], without adding a moment assumption. The geometric existence and coalescence part of their Lemma 4.6 is unchanged. Symmetry of \\(\\mathcal C\\) follows also from its deterministic realization as the set of ray functionals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.5, “Uniform metric approximation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under the iid assumptions of Proposition 3, almost surely the two-endpoint approximation (5) holds uniformly over \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Under the iid assumptions of Proposition 3, almost surely the two-endpoint approximation (5) holds uniformly over \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The extended shape theorem [AH19, Proposition 3.4] states that, for each \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), almost surely there is \\(N&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n|T(z,z+v)-m(v)|\n\\le\\varepsilon\\max\\{|z|,|v|\\}\n\\qquad(|z|\\ge N,\\ v\\in\\mathbb Z^2).\n\\]\nGiven \\(x,y\\), take \\(z\\) to be the endpoint with larger norm and \\(v\\) the displacement to the other endpoint. Then\n\\[\n|z|\\ge\\frac{|x|+|y|}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\max\\{|z|,|v|\\}\\le|x|+|y|.\n\\]\nSymmetry of \\(T\\) and \\(m\\) proves the claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Corollary 2.4, “Exclusion of deterministic labels.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each fixed \\(\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\), almost surely no bigeodesic has an end of label \\(\\rho\\).", "statement": "<p>For each fixed \\(\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\), almost surely no bigeodesic has an end of label \\(\\rho\\). The same holds simultaneously for every member of any countable deterministic subset of \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose a bigeodesic has a forward end of label \\(\\rho\\). The forward ray starting at any backward vertex has that same label by tail invariance. Fixed-label uniqueness from Lemma 3.1 identifies these rays with \\(g_\\rho(x)\\). The middle vertex would belong to the family from infinitely many starting vertices, contrary to backward finiteness. The final assertion follows by countable intersection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Sector geometry, ordering, and uniform confinement.", "statement": "<p>**Sector geometry, ordering, and uniform confinement. Sources: Lemmas 3.1–3.3 and Proposition 3.4.**</p><p>Work under Theorem 1 on a common event satisfying Propositions 1 and 3, imposing fixed-label assertions for each countable deterministic collection of labels specified below.</p><p>For an oriented bigeodesic through \\(z\\), let \\(p^+\\) and \\(p^-\\) denote its two rays from \\(z\\). Then\n\\[\nB_{p^+}(z,w)+B_{p^-}(z,w)\\le0\n\\qquad(w\\in\\mathbb Z^2), \\tag{6}\n\\]\nand \\(\\rho_{p^-}=-\\rho_{p^+}\\). Moving the base point along the bigeodesic changes neither end label nor end Busemann function.</p><p>There are a countable deterministic symmetric set \\(D\\subset\\mathcal C\\) and countably many charts. A chart consists of lattice coordinates and a compact arc \\(J\\subset\\mathcal S\\) with distinct endpoints \\(\\rho_\\ell,\\rho_r\\in\\mathcal C\\), such that, with \\(I=\\mathcal C\\cap J\\),\n\\[\nd_2\\ge c&gt;0\n\\qquad(\\rho\\in J,\\ d\\in A(\\rho)), \\tag{7}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr(\\rho)=\\rho(e_1)\n\\]\nis injective on \\(J\\), increasing from \\(\\rho_\\ell\\) to \\(\\rho_r\\). Every member of \\(\\mathcal C\\setminus D\\) belongs to the relative interior of a chart. Almost surely every bigeodesic can therefore be oriented with upper label in a chart interior and lower label its negative.</p><p>Fix one chart. Upper rays have labels in \\(I\\), lower rays labels in \\(-I\\). Their heights tend respectively to \\(+\\infty\\) and \\(-\\infty\\). Rays with disjoint tails have a stable left-to-right order at first arrivals on sufficiently distant horizontal rows. If an upper ray \\(p\\) is left of an upper ray \\(q\\), then\n\\[\nr(\\rho_p)\\le r(\\rho_q). \\tag{8}\n\\]\nDistinct labels give disjoint tails and strict order. The lower version uses increasing horizontal coordinate of the lower labels.</p><p>Choose \\(s&gt;0\\) so that every upper contact satisfies \\(d_2&gt;2s|d_1|\\), and every lower contact satisfies \\(-d_2&gt;2s|d_1|\\). Define\n\\[\nQ^\\pm(z,H)\n=\\{w\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\ \\pm(w_2-z_2)\\ge s|w_1-z_1|-H\\}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nAlmost surely there is a finite \\(H(z)\\ge1\\) for every \\(z\\) such that every upper ray from \\(z\\) lies in \\(Q^+(z,H(z))\\), and every lower ray lies in \\(Q^-(z,H(z))\\). Moreover, for every fixed \\(L&lt;\\infty\\),\n\\[\n\\max_{|z|\\le Ln}H(z)=o(n). \\tag{12}\n\\]\nThese assertions are simultaneous over every ray in the chart.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.3 prove the opposite-label, chart, and ordering assertions. It remains to prove uniform confinement, following Proposition 3.4 of the paper.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\{\\rho_\\ell,\\rho_r,-\\rho_\\ell,-\\rho_r\\}.\n\\]\nFor these four deterministic labels, Proposition 3 gives unique rays \\(g_z^\\lambda\\) from every \\(z\\) and common Busemann functions \\(B_\\lambda\\). Let \\(\\sigma_\\lambda=1\\) for the upper endpoints and \\(-1\\) for the lower endpoints. Define\n\\[\nH(z)\n=1+\\max_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda}\\sup_{w\\in g_z^\\lambda}\n\\bigl(s|w_1-z_1|-\\sigma_\\lambda(w_2-z_2)\\bigr)_+. \\tag{13}\n\\]\nEach supremum is finite: limiting directions lie strictly inside the cone of slope \\(2s\\), so outside a finite prefix the positive part vanishes.</p><p>Put\n\\[\na_\\lambda(x)=B_\\lambda(0,x)-\\lambda(x)=o(|x|).\n\\]\nCalibration yields, for \\(w\\in g_z^\\lambda\\),\n\\[\nT(z,w)=B_\\lambda(z,w)\n=\\lambda(w-z)+a_\\lambda(w)-a_\\lambda(z). \\tag{14}\n\\]\nFix \\(L\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). If \\(|z|\\le Ln\\) and \\(|w-z|\\ge\\eta n\\), then\n\\[\n|z|+|w|\\le2Ln+|w-z|\n\\le(1+2L/\\eta)|w-z|.\n\\]\nThe uniform metric approximation, the four rooted asymptotics, and (14) imply\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\substack{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda,\\ |z|\\le Ln,\\ w\\in g_z^\\lambda\\\\\n|w-z|\\ge\\eta n}}\n\\frac{m(w-z)-\\lambda(w-z)}{|w-z|}\n\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{15}\n\\]\nExplicitly, bound each rooted error by \\(\\varepsilon|x|+K_\\varepsilon\\) and the metric error by \\(\\varepsilon(|z|+|w|)+K_\\varepsilon\\). The quotient is at most\n\\[\n2\\varepsilon(1+2L/\\eta)+\\frac{3K_\\varepsilon}{\\eta n};\n\\]\ntake \\(n\\to\\infty\\), then \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>For each \\(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda\\), \\(m(d)-\\lambda(d)\\) has a positive minimum on\n\\[\n\\{d\\in S^1:\\sigma_\\lambda d_2\\le s|d_1|\\}.\n\\]\nAll its zeros lie strictly inside the cone of slope \\(2s\\). Thus, eventually, every endpoint-ray displacement of length at least \\(\\eta n\\) lies in the unshifted cone of slope \\(s\\). For shorter displacements,\n\\[\n\\bigl(s|d_1|-\\sigma_\\lambda d_2\\bigr)_+\n\\le(s+1)|d|&lt;(s+1)\\eta n.\n\\]\nHence the maximum in (12) is eventually at most \\(1+(s+1)\\eta n\\). Letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) proves (12).</p><p>Now fix \\(z\\) and an upper ray \\(p\\) with\n\\[\nr(\\rho_\\ell)&lt;r(\\rho_p)&lt;r(\\rho_r).\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\na=g_z^{\\rho_\\ell},\\qquad b=g_z^{\\rho_r},\n\\qquad Q=Q^+(z,H(z)).\n\\]\nThe bounding rays lie in \\(Q\\). They share a finite initial portion ending at \\(v\\), then have disjoint tails: a reunion after splitting would violate finite uniqueness, and their distinct labels prevent an infinite common prefix.</p><p>Suppose \\(p\\) visits \\(w\\notin Q\\). Choose an integer \\(N\\) above the common prefix, above the prefix of \\(p\\) ending at \\(w\\), and high enough that all three pairwise orders have stabilized. Their first arrivals satisfy\n\\[\n(a_N)_1&lt;(p_N)_1&lt;(b_N)_1.\n\\]\nThe two arcs \\(a[v,a_N]\\), \\(b[v,b_N]\\), together with the horizontal segment \\([a_N,b_N]\\), form a simple closed polygonal curve \\(\\mathcal J\\). Its bounded Jordan domain \\(D\\) lies in \\(Q\\): the boundary lies in \\(Q\\), which is convex, and a bounded Jordan domain lies in the convex hull of its boundary.</p><p>The common prefix from \\(z\\) to \\(v\\) need not lie in \\(D\\). Nevertheless, the last edge into \\(p_N\\) comes vertically from below, so the portion sufficiently close to \\(p_N\\) lies inside \\(D\\). Starting from \\(w\\notin Q\\), the ray must enter \\(D\\) before its first row-\\(N\\) visit. It cannot enter through the top segment. It therefore meets one of the bounding arcs at a lattice vertex \\(u\\), possibly \\(u=v\\), after visiting \\(w\\). The prefix \\(p[z,u]\\) and the corresponding bounding prefix are both finite geodesics, so uniqueness identifies them. But the latter lies in \\(Q\\), while the former contains \\(w\\notin Q\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus all intermediate upper rays lie in \\(Q\\). Endpoint-label rays are the unique endpoint rays. Reflection gives the lower assertion. The reasoning is pathwise on one common event and applies to arbitrary rays, establishing the simultaneous conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Opposite functionals.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 4, the two rays \\(p^+,p^-\\) of an oriented bigeodesic through \\(z\\) satisfy (6), have opposite labels, and retain their labels and Busemann functions when the base point moves along the …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 4, the two rays \\(p^+,p^-\\) of an oriented bigeodesic through \\(z\\) satisfy (6), have opposite labels, and retain their labels and Busemann functions when the base point moves along the bigeodesic.</p><p>For every countable deterministic \\(D\\subset\\mathcal C\\), almost surely no bigeodesic has an end label in \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For vertices \\(p_i^+\\) and \\(p_j^-\\) on opposite arms, optimality and the triangle inequality give\n\\[\nT(z,p_i^+)+T(z,p_j^-)\n=T(p_i^+,p_j^-)\n\\le T(w,p_i^+)+T(w,p_j^-).\n\\]\nSubtract the right-hand terms and let \\(i,j\\to\\infty\\). This gives (6).</p><p>By additivity and asymptotic linearity, its left side is\n\\[\n(\\rho_{p^+}+\\rho_{p^-})(w-z)+o(|w|).\n\\]\nPut \\(w=z+ne_k\\) and \\(w=z-ne_k\\), divide by \\(n\\), and let \\(n\\to\\infty\\), for \\(k=1,2\\). The sum of the functionals vanishes.</p><p>Moving the base point is handled by tail invariance from Proposition 1. The deterministic-label exclusion is Proposition 3.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Countably many charts.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are a deterministic countable set \\(D\\subset\\mathcal C\\), symmetric under negation, and a countable family of charts satisfying the chart assertions in Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>There are a deterministic countable set \\(D\\subset\\mathcal C\\), symmetric under negation, and a countable family of charts satisfying the chart assertions in Proposition 4. Almost surely every bigeodesic admits the upper/lower orientation described there.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nK=\\{x:m(x)\\le1\\},\n\\qquad\nK^*=\\{\\rho:\\rho(x)\\le m(x)\\text{ for all }x\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\rho\\in\\partial K^*\\), the exposed face\n\\[\nF_\\rho=\\{x\\in K:\\rho(x)=1\\}\n\\]\nis a point or a line segment. Its radial image is \\(A(\\rho)\\). If this image contains two directions, it is a nondegenerate closed circular arc. The relative interiors of these arcs are disjoint for distinct \\(\\rho\\): a direction in such an interior corresponds to the relative interior of a nondegenerate face of \\(K\\), whose supporting line and normalized functional are unique. Every nondegenerate arc contains a rational angle. Thus only countably many functionals have more than one contact direction.</p><p>There are also only countably many members of \\(\\mathcal C\\) isolated on at least one circular side. They are endpoints of components of \\(\\partial K^*\\setminus\\mathcal C\\), with finite-set cases included. The open components are disjoint and each contains a member of a fixed countable dense subset of the circle.</p><p>For a remaining \\(\\rho\\), write \\(A(\\rho)=\\{d\\}\\); \\(\\mathcal C\\) accumulates at \\(\\rho\\) from both sides. A lattice rotation makes \\(d_2&gt;0\\). The contact correspondence is upper semicontinuous: if \\(\\rho_n\\to\\rho\\), \\(d_n\\in A(\\rho_n)\\), and \\(d_n\\to d'\\), then \\(m(d')=\\rho(d')\\). Compactness therefore supplies a boundary neighborhood of \\(\\rho\\) on which all contacts have second coordinate at least some \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p>Choose a countable dense subset \\(D_0\\) of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Choose a point of \\(D_0\\) on either side of \\(\\rho\\) within that neighborhood. Their compact joining arc through \\(\\rho\\) can be taken inside it. There are countably many pairs from \\(D_0\\) and finitely many lattice coordinate choices. These give the countable chart family. Let \\(D\\) contain \\(D_0\\), the two exceptional countable sets just described, and their negatives.</p><p>If chart points \\(\\rho,\\lambda\\) had equal horizontal coordinate and \\(\\lambda(e_2)&gt;\\rho(e_2)\\), choose \\(d\\in A(\\rho)\\), with \\(d_2&gt;0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\lambda(d)&gt;\\rho(d)=m(d),\n\\]\ncontrary to \\(\\lambda\\in K^*\\). The same argument shows each chart point is the top endpoint of its vertical section of \\(K^*\\). Thus the continuous horizontal coordinate is injective and strictly monotone on the arc; orient the arc so it increases. On the opposite lower arc, horizontal coordinate again increases left to right, and negation reverses the corresponding label order.</p><p>Lemma 4.1 excludes \\(D\\) simultaneously and identifies the opposite label. The contact restriction makes upper-ray height tend to \\(+\\infty\\) and lower-ray height to \\(-\\infty\\), giving the orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Local ray order.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix a chart as in Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart as in Proposition 4. Two upper rays with disjoint tails have a stable left-to-right order at their first visits to rows \\(\\{x_2=N\\}\\) as \\(N\\to\\infty\\). If \\(p\\) is left of \\(q\\), then (8) holds. Distinct labels give disjoint tails and strictly ordered first arrivals in increasing \\(r\\)-order.</p><p>For lower rays the same holds as \\(N\\to-\\infty\\), using horizontal coordinate of the lower functional.</p>", "proof": "<p>If two rays meet infinitely often, choose a shared vertex beyond their finite initial portions. The geodesic segments from it to every later common vertex agree by finite uniqueness. These common vertices escape to infinity, so the tails agree. Tail invariance gives equal labels. Thus distinct labels have only finitely many common vertices.</p><p>For disjoint tails, choose a height \\(h\\) above both starting sites, all intersections, and finite prefixes preceding the disjoint tails. After their last visits to row \\(h\\), both rays stay strictly above it. For large \\(N\\), their first row-\\(N\\) visits occur after these last visits. The intervening disjoint strip crossings have the same endpoint order on both boundary lines, by the Jordan curve theorem. This proves stabilization.</p><p>Assume the stable order is \\(p\\) left of \\(q\\). Put\n\\[\nu=(-n,0),\\qquad v=(n,0).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\gamma_j\\) be the walk formed by a finite geodesic from \\(v\\) to \\(p_j\\) followed by the tail of \\(p\\). Define\n\\[\n\\delta_j=T(v,p_j)-B_p(v,p_j)\\ge0.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, \\(\\delta_j\\to0\\). Every vertex \\(w\\) of \\(\\gamma_j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n0\\le T(v,w)-B_p(v,w)\\le\\delta_j. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nOn its initial geodesic this follows by decomposing cost and Busemann increment at \\(w\\); on the tail it follows from the triangle inequality and exact calibration. Define \\(\\xi_j\\) by joining \\(u\\) to \\(q_j\\) and following \\(q\\), with error \\(\\delta'_j\\to0\\).</p><p>Horizontal directions are outside both contact sets, so\n\\[\n\\gamma\n=\\min_{\\lambda\\in\\{\\rho_p,\\rho_q\\},\\ \\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}}\n\\bigl(m(\\sigma e_1)-\\lambda(\\sigma e_1)\\bigr)&gt;0.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\na_p(x)=B_p(0,x)-\\rho_p(x),\n\\qquad\na_q(x)=B_q(0,x)-\\rho_q(x).\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the uniform metric estimate and the two rooted asymptotics give \\(K_\\varepsilon&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n|T(x,y)-m(y-x)|\\le\\varepsilon(|x|+|y|)+K_\\varepsilon,\n\\]\n\\[\n|a_p(x)|+|a_q(x)|\\le\\varepsilon|x|+K_\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nFor \\(w=(t,0)\\) with \\(d=|w-v|\\ge n/2\\),\n\\[\n|v|+|w|\\le2n+d\\le5d.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nT(v,w)-B_p(v,w)\n\\ge(\\gamma-10\\varepsilon)d-3K_\\varepsilon. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe identical estimate holds for \\(u,q\\). Choose \\(\\varepsilon&lt;\\gamma/40\\), then \\(n\\) so large that \\(3K_\\varepsilon\\le\\gamma n/8\\). The right side of (10) is at least \\(\\gamma d/2\\ge\\gamma n/4\\). For this fixed \\(n\\), take \\(j\\) large enough that both errors are smaller than \\(\\gamma n/4\\). Every row-zero visit of \\(\\gamma_j\\) then has first coordinate in \\((n/2,3n/2)\\), and every such visit of \\(\\xi_j\\) lies in \\((-3n/2,-n/2)\\).</p><p>Both walks eventually follow their upward tails. Their last row-zero visits have order \\(\\xi_j,\\gamma_j\\). Choose row \\(N\\) above the joining paths, discarded original prefixes, and prefixes ending at these last visits, and high enough for the stable order of \\(p,q\\). The first row-\\(N\\) arrivals have the opposite order \\(\\gamma_j,\\xi_j\\). The strip-crossing fact gives a common lattice vertex \\(w_j\\).</p><p>At \\(w_j\\), metric domination and (9) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_p(u,v)\n&amp;=B_p(u,w_j)-B_p(v,w_j)\\\\\n&amp;\\le T(u,w_j)-T(v,w_j)+\\delta_j\\\\\n&amp;\\le B_q(u,w_j)-B_q(v,w_j)+\\delta_j+\\delta'_j\\\\\n&amp;=B_q(u,v)+\\delta_j+\\delta'_j.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nLet \\(j\\to\\infty\\) with \\(n\\) fixed. Then \\(B_p(u,v)\\le B_q(u,v)\\). Rooted asymptotics give\n\\[\nB_p(u,v)=2n\\rho_p(e_1)+o(n),\n\\qquad\nB_q(u,v)=2n\\rho_q(e_1)+o(n).\n\\]\nDivide by \\(2n\\). This proves (8), and injectivity of \\(r\\) gives strictness for distinct labels.</p><p>Reflection in the horizontal axis proves the lower version; it preserves \\(e_1\\) and therefore the horizontal parameter.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, “Crossing from separated sites.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), its horizontal order \\(r\\), and its cone slope \\(s\\) from Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), its horizontal order \\(r\\), and its cone slope \\(s\\) from Proposition 4. Let \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\) satisfy \\(x_1&lt;y_1\\), and let upper rays \\(p,q\\), starting respectively at \\(x,y\\), satisfy\n\\[\np\\subset Q^+(x,H_x),\\qquad q\\subset Q^+(y,H_y).\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\ns(y_1-x_1)&gt;H_x+H_y+|x_2-y_2| \\tag{16}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr(\\rho_p)&gt;r(\\rho_q),\n\\]\nthen \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) intersect.</p><p>The same holds for lower rays in their lower cones, using the horizontal coordinates of their lower labels.</p><p>In particular, for fixed vertices \\(u,v\\) and \\(y_N=(N,h)\\) on a fixed horizontal row, Proposition 4 gives \\(H(y_N)=o(N)\\). Therefore, for all sufficiently large \\(N\\), this crossing criterion applies simultaneously to every reverse-ordered pair of chart rays from \\(u,y_N\\), and from \\(v,y_N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For upper rays put \\(h_0=\\max(x_2,y_2)\\). Both rays have last visits to row \\(h_0\\), since they tend upward. Call the last vertices \\(\\widehat x,\\widehat y\\). The cone bounds give\n\\[\n|\\widehat x_1-x_1|\\le\\frac{h_0-x_2+H_x}{s},\n\\qquad\n|\\widehat y_1-y_1|\\le\\frac{h_0-y_2+H_y}{s}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n(h_0-x_2)+(h_0-y_2)=|x_2-y_2|,\n\\]\ncondition (16) implies \\(\\widehat x_1&lt;\\widehat y_1\\).</p><p>If the rays did not intersect, their portions from these last visits to their first visits to a sufficiently high row would be disjoint strip crossings. Their order would remain \\(p,q\\), contrary to the strict reverse label order and Proposition 4.</p><p>For lower rays use \\(h_0=\\min(x_2,y_2)\\) and last visits before departure toward height \\(-\\infty\\). The same estimates and lower ordering apply.</p><p>For the final assertion, the fixed offsets at \\(u,v\\), the \\(o(N)\\) offset at \\(y_N\\), and bounded height differences are eventually dominated by the horizontal separations in (16).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 4.2, “Countability of exceptional labels.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\) and work in an environment satisfying the simultaneous structural, uniform metric, and all-ray confinement conclusions of Propositions 3 and 4.", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\) and work in an environment satisfying the simultaneous structural, uniform metric, and all-ray confinement conclusions of Propositions 3 and 4.</p><p>Call \\(\\rho\\in I\\) exceptional if either:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. two distinct rays of label \\(\\rho\\) start at the same vertex; or</p><p class=\"li num\">2. two rays of label \\(\\rho\\) have different Busemann functions.</p><p>Write \\(E_I(\\omega)\\) for the set of such labels.</p><p>Almost surely \\(E_I(\\omega)\\) is at most countable. The same holds with \\(I\\) replaced by \\(-I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First consider branching. Fix \\(x\\) and distinct first edges \\(e,f\\) at \\(x\\). At most two labels in \\(I\\) can each be realized by a ray starting with \\(e\\) and by a ray starting with \\(f\\).</p><p>Otherwise choose three such labels \\(\\rho_1,\\rho_2,\\rho_3\\) in strict \\(r\\)-order and their six rays. Geodesics from \\(x\\) form a tree: after diverging they cannot reunite, by finite uniqueness. The two first-edge branches are disjoint away from \\(x\\).</p><p>Choose a row high enough that the six first arrivals are distinct and ordered by label. Color each arrival according to branch \\(e\\) or \\(f\\). The resulting word consists of three consecutive two-letter groups, each containing both colors. It has at least three color changes and therefore an alternating four-letter subsequence.</p><p>Join the two \\(e\\)-colored endpoints of this subsequence by their unique arc in the \\(e\\)-branch, and do the same in the \\(f\\)-branch. These arcs avoid \\(x\\), are disjoint, and have interiors strictly below the row. Their endpoints alternate along the row, contradicting the Jordan curve theorem. This proves the claim for \\(x,e,f\\).</p><p>Two distinct rays from one vertex have a first divergence vertex. Countable union over vertices and first-edge pairs proves countability for condition 1.</p><p>For Busemann nonuniqueness, first take \\(x,y\\) with \\(y\\) far enough to the right that Proposition 5 applies to every reverse-ordered pair of upper rays from them. If \\(B,B'\\) are Busemann functions of upper rays with labels \\(\\rho,\\sigma\\) and \\(r(\\rho)&lt;r(\\sigma)\\), then\n\\[\nB(x,y)\\le B'(x,y). \\tag{19}\n\\]\nIndeed, Lemma 6.1 supplies a \\(B\\)-calibrated ray from \\(y\\) and a \\(B'\\)-calibrated ray from \\(x\\). They have labels \\(\\rho,\\sigma\\) and therefore intersect at some \\(w\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(x,y)\n&amp;=B(x,w)-T(y,w)\\\\\n&amp;\\le T(x,w)-T(y,w)\\\\\n&amp;\\le T(x,w)-B'(y,w)\n=B'(x,y).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>For such \\(x,y\\), define\n\\[\nS_\\rho(x,y)=\\{B_p(x,y):p\\text{ is a ray of label }\\rho\\}.\n\\]\nThese sets lie in \\([-T(x,y),T(x,y)]\\). Inequality (19) gives\n\\[\n\\sup S_\\rho(x,y)\\le\\inf S_\\sigma(x,y)\n\\quad\\text{when }r(\\rho)&lt;r(\\sigma). \\tag{20}\n\\]\nThus the nonempty open intervals\n\\[\n(\\inf S_\\rho(x,y),\\sup S_\\rho(x,y))\n\\]\nare pairwise disjoint. Each contains a rational, so only countably many labels have multiple values at this pair.</p><p>To detect an arbitrary disagreement, fix \\(u,v\\). Proposition 4 controls all upper rays from \\(u,v\\) by finite offsets and all rays from \\(y_N=(N,0)\\) by offsets \\(o(N)\\). For sufficiently large integer \\(N\\), both pairs \\((u,y_N)\\), \\((v,y_N)\\) satisfy (20) for all labels and Busemann functions.</p><p>If \\(B,B'\\) of the same label disagree at \\((u,v)\\), additivity in\n\\[\nB(u,v)=B(u,y_N)-B(v,y_N),\n\\qquad\nB'(u,v)=B'(u,y_N)-B'(v,y_N)\n\\]\nforces disagreement at one of these comparison pairs. The labels disagreeing at \\((u,v)\\) lie in a union of two countable sets. Countable union over \\(u,v\\) proves countability for condition 2.</p><p>The integer \\(N\\) may depend on the environment and \\(u,v\\); only all-ray cone bounds were used. Reflection proves the lower-sector assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1, “Rays calibrated from an arbitrary vertex.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(p\\) be a ray, put \\(B=B_p\\), and let \\(x\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p\\) be a ray, put \\(B=B_p\\), and let \\(x\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\). Under the structural assumptions of Proposition 6, there is a ray \\(q=(q_j)_{j\\ge0}\\) from \\(x\\) such that\n\\[\nT(x,q_j)=B(x,q_j)\\qquad(j\\ge0). \\tag{17}\n\\]\nThe ray \\(q\\) has the same label as \\(p\\). The conclusion does not in general assert \\(B_q=B_p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take finite geodesics from \\(x\\) to \\(p_k\\). Since \\(p\\) is simple, \\(|p_k|\\to\\infty\\), and their lengths tend to infinity. Local finiteness and a diagonal subsequence give a local limit \\(q\\) from \\(x\\). Each finite prefix is eventually a prefix of these geodesics, so \\(q\\) is a simple infinite geodesic.</p><p>By Proposition 1,\n\\[\nd_k(x):=T(x,p_k)-B(x,p_k)\\ge0,\n\\qquad d_k(x)\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{18}\n\\]\nIf \\(w\\) lies on the geodesic from \\(x\\) to \\(p_k\\), then\n\\[\n0\\le T(x,w)-B(x,w)\\le d_k(x),\n\\]\nbecause \\(T(w,p_k)\\ge B(w,p_k)\\). Passing to the local limit proves (17).</p><p>For every \\(w\\),\n\\[\nT(x,q_j)-T(w,q_j)\n\\le B(x,q_j)-B(w,q_j)=B(x,w).\n\\]\nThus \\(B_q(x,w)\\le B(x,w)\\). Write \\(\\sigma=\\rho_q\\), \\(\\rho=\\rho_p\\). Asymptotic linearity at \\(w=ke_i\\) and \\(w=-ke_i\\), for \\(i=1,2\\), gives both\n\\[\n\\sigma(e_i)\\le\\rho(e_i),\n\\qquad\n\\sigma(e_i)\\ge\\rho(e_i).\n\\]\nHence \\(\\sigma=\\rho\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 4.5, “Coalescence at opposite exceptional labels.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), and let \\(U\\) have the iid law \\(\\mathbb P\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), and let \\(U\\) have the iid law \\(\\mathbb P\\). Define \\(E_I(U)\\) as in Proposition 6.</p><p>Almost surely, simultaneously for all \\(\\rho\\in E_I(U)\\), every two \\(U\\)-rays of label \\(-\\rho\\) coalesce. The same holds with upper and lower sectors exchanged. Both conclusions hold simultaneously for the countable collection of charts from Proposition 4.</p><p>For the proof, for \\(n\\ge1\\) set\n\\[\nH_n=\\{z:z_2=-n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E_n^+\n=\\{\\{x,y\\}:x\\sim y,\\ x_2\\ge-n,\\ y_2\\ge-n\\}.\n\\]\nTake independent iid arrays \\(A^{(n)},D^{(n)}\\), all independent of \\(U\\), and define\n\\[\n(V_n)_e=\n\\begin{cases}\nU_e,&amp;e\\in\\mathcal E_n^+,\\\\\nA_e^{(n)},&amp;e\\notin\\mathcal E_n^+,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n(W_n)_e=\n\\begin{cases}\nD_e^{(n)},&amp;e\\in\\mathcal E_n^+,\\\\\nU_e,&amp;e\\notin\\mathcal E_n^+.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{24}\n\\]\nFor each \\(n\\), \\((V_n,W_n)\\) has law \\(\\mathbb P\\otimes\\mathbb P\\). No independence across different \\(n\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every \\(n\\), apply Lemma 7.1 to the independent pair \\((V_n,W_n)\\). Take the countable intersection of these events and the event in Lemma 7.2.</p><p>Fix any \\(\\rho\\in E_I(U)\\) and two upper rays witnessing exceptionality. For a branching witness, Lemma 7.2 preserves the distinct paths, common starting vertex, and exact label at every sufficiently deep subsequence cut. For a Busemann-disagreement witness, include a pair \\(x,y\\) of unequal evaluations in its finite witness set. Again every sufficiently deep subsequence cut preserves the exact label and unequal evaluations. Choose such a cut \\(n\\). Then \\(\\rho\\in E_I(V_n)\\).</p><p>Let \\(q,q'\\) be arbitrary lower \\(U\\)-rays of label \\(-\\rho\\). Lemma 7.2, for this fixed \\(n\\), gives tails that are \\(W_n\\)-rays of the same exact label. Lemma 7.1 makes them coalesce, so the original \\(U\\)-rays coalesce.</p><p>All choices were pathwise after fixing one common event, so the conclusion is simultaneous over \\(\\rho\\) and its witnesses. Its failure is the analytic four-witness event with both environments set equal to \\(U\\), and is measurable in the completed marginal space. Reflection exchanges upper and lower; countable intersection gives the conclusion in all charts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “Countable exceptional parameters in an independent metric.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(V,W\\) be independent iid environments.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(V,W\\) be independent iid environments. Almost surely, simultaneously for every \\(\\rho\\in E_I(V)\\), all \\(W\\)-rays of label \\(-\\rho\\) coalesce.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the standard Borel configuration space \\(\\Omega\\) before completion and path space\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=(\\mathbb Z^2)^{\\mathbb N_0}.\n\\]\nLet \\(R(\\omega,p,\\rho)\\) assert that \\(p\\) is a simple ray in \\(\\omega\\), its Busemann limits exist, and its Busemann function is asymptotically linear to \\(\\rho\\). This is Borel by Proposition 1. Define Busemann evaluations as zero where limits do not exist; \\(R\\) explicitly requires existence.</p><p>For two paths satisfying \\(R(\\omega,p_i,\\rho)\\), exceptionality is witnessed by the Borel disjunction\n\\[\n[p_{1,0}=p_{2,0}\\text{ and }p_1\\ne p_2]\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n[\\exists x,y:\\ B_{p_1}(x,y)\\ne B_{p_2}(x,y)]. \\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe common-tail test\n\\[\n\\exists a,b\\in\\mathbb N_0\\ \\forall k\\in\\mathbb N_0:\nq_{1,a+k}=q_{2,b+k} \\tag{22}\n\\]\nis also Borel.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal J\\subset\\Omega^2\\) be the set admitting one \\(\\rho\\in I\\) and four paths \\(p_1,p_2,q_1,q_2\\) such that \\(p_1,p_2\\) satisfy \\(R(v,p_i,\\rho)\\) and (21), while \\(q_1,q_2\\) satisfy \\(R(w,q_i,-\\rho)\\) and fail (22). This is a projection of a Borel relation and is analytic, hence measurable in the completed product.</p><p>For every \\(v\\) in the full-measure countability event of Proposition 6,\n\\[\n\\mathcal J_v\n=\\bigcup_{\\rho\\in E_I(v)}\n\\{w:\\text{two \\(w\\)-rays of label \\(-\\rho\\) do not coalesce}\\}. \\tag{23}\n\\]\nFor this fixed \\(v\\), the labels in the countable union are fixed parameters. Symmetry gives \\(-\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\); Proposition 3 makes each event null. Thus \\(\\mathbb P(\\mathcal J_v)=0\\) for almost every \\(v\\).</p><p>Completed-product Fubini gives\n\\[\n(\\mathbb P\\otimes\\mathbb P)(\\mathcal J)=0.\n\\]\nEquivalently, replace \\(\\mathcal J\\) by a Borel set agreeing with it outside a Borel product-null set and apply ordinary Fubini. No measurable enumeration of \\(E_I(v)\\) is needed. Universal all-ray conditions are imposed outside the four-path projection.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.4, “Half-plane transfer.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the coupling (24), there is a deterministic increasing sequence \\(n_k\\to\\infty\\) and an event of probability one such that:", "statement": "<p>For the coupling (24), there is a deterministic increasing sequence \\(n_k\\to\\infty\\) and an event of probability one such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every finite collection of upper \\(U\\)-rays and finite \\(K\\subset\\mathbb Z^2\\), all sufficiently large \\(k\\) preserve every ray in \\(V_{n_k}\\), preserve its exact label, and satisfy\n   \\[\n   B_p^{V_{n_k}}(x,y)=B_p^U(x,y)\\qquad(x,y\\in K).\n   \\]\n   More precisely, for each such \\(k,p\\),\n   \\[\n   T_{V_{n_k}}(x,p_j)=T_U(x,p_j)\n   \\]\n   for all sufficiently large \\(j\\), simultaneously for \\(x\\in K\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For each fixed \\(n\\), every lower \\(U\\)-ray has a tail that is a \\(W_n\\)-ray with exactly its original label.</p><p>Thresholds may depend on the rays, labels, finite set \\(K\\), and, in assertion 2, on \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a common event on which all-ray asymptotics, finite geodesic existence and uniqueness, and the uniform metric estimate hold in \\(U\\) and all \\(V_n,W_n\\).</p><p>For any metric \\(T\\), put\n\\[\nE_n(T)\n=\\sup_{x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^2}\n\\frac{|T(x,y)-m(y-x)|}{|x|+|y|+n}. \\tag{25}\n\\]\nFor a fixed typical metric, \\(E_n(T)\\to0\\): the uniform estimate bounds the numerator by \\(\\varepsilon(|x|+|y|)\\) outside finitely many pairs, and the remaining maximum divided by \\(n\\) tends to zero.</p><p>Since every \\(V_n\\) has the iid law, \\(E_n(T_{V_n})\\to0\\) in probability. Choose increasing deterministic \\(n_k\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(E_{n_k}(T_{V_{n_k}})&gt;2^{-k})&lt;2^{-k}.\n\\]\nBorel–Cantelli gives\n\\[\nE_{n_k}(T_{V_{n_k}})\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{26}\n\\]\nThis is the only subsequence used.</p><p>Fix an arbitrary upper \\(U\\)-ray \\(p\\) of label \\(\\rho\\in I\\), and write\n\\[\na(w)=B_p^U(0,w)-\\rho(w)=o(|w|),\n\\qquad\nB_p^U(x,z)=\\rho(z-x)+a(z)-a(x). \\tag{27}\n\\]\nSince all contacts of \\(\\rho\\) are strictly upper,\n\\[\n\\gamma=\\min_{|d|=1,\\ d_2\\le0}(m(d)-\\rho(d))&gt;0. \\tag{28}\n\\]\nEnlarge \\(K\\) to contain \\(p_0\\), and let \\(R_K=\\max_{x\\in K}|x|\\). For \\(n\\ge2R_K+1\\), \\(x\\in K\\), and \\(z\\in H_n\\),\n\\[\n|z-x|\\ge|z|/2,\n\\qquad\n|x|+|z|+n\\le\\tfrac52|z|\\le5|z-x|. \\tag{29}\n\\]\nThus, for \\(n=n_k\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{V_n}(x,z)-B_p^U(x,z)\n\\ge{}&amp;\\gamma|z-x|\\\\\n&amp;-E_n(T_{V_n})(|x|+|z|+n)-|a(z)|-|a(x)|.\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(30)\n\\]\nBy (26), (29), and \\(a(z)=o(|z|)\\), all errors are \\(o(|z-x|)\\), uniformly over \\(x\\in K,z\\in H_n\\). Consequently, for all sufficiently large \\(k\\),\n\\[\nT_{V_{n_k}}(x,z)-B_p^U(x,z)\n\\ge\\frac\\gamma2|z-x|\\ge\\frac\\gamma4n_k. \\tag{31}\n\\]\nThe same estimate holds for \\(U\\), since \\(E_n(T_U)\\to0\\).</p><p>The entire ray lies strictly above \\(H_n\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\). If a walk from \\(x\\) to \\(p_j\\), with endpoints above the cut, touches \\(H_n\\), let \\(z\\) be its last cut vertex. Its suffix uses shared edges, so its \\(V_n\\)-cost is at least\n\\[\nT_{V_n}(x,z)+B_p^U(z,p_j)\n=B_p^U(x,p_j)+[T_{V_n}(x,z)-B_p^U(x,z)]. \\tag{32}\n\\]\nRepeated cut visits and repeated edges cause no problem. The same comparison holds in \\(U\\).</p><p>For \\(x=p_0\\), the original segment has unchanged cost \\(B_p^U(p_0,p_j)\\). Inequality (31) excludes cut-touching competitors; cut-avoiding competitors retain their \\(U\\)-cost. Thus \\(p\\) is a \\(V_{n_k}\\)-ray for all sufficiently large \\(k\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(x\\in K\\),\n\\[\nT_U(x,p_j)-B_p^U(x,p_j)\\to0.\n\\]\nFix sufficiently large \\(k\\). Eventually this defect is smaller than \\(\\gamma n_k/4\\). The \\(U\\)-version of (32) forces a \\(U\\)-geodesic to avoid the cut, providing an unchanged competitor in \\(V_{n_k}\\). The \\(V_{n_k}\\)-version excludes a cut-touching improvement. Therefore\n\\[\nT_{V_{n_k}}(x,p_j)=T_U(x,p_j)\n\\quad\\text{for all sufficiently large }j. \\tag{33}\n\\]\nOne threshold works for finite \\(K\\). Subtracting distances and taking limits preserves the specified Busemann evaluations.</p><p>To identify the exact label at a fixed preserved cut \\(n=n_k\\), fix \\(0&lt;c&lt;1\\), take \\(x_2\\ge c|x|\\), and let \\(z\\in H_n\\). For \\(d=z-x\\),\n\\[\n|d|\\ge x_2+n\\ge c|x|,\n\\qquad |z|\\le|d|+|x|.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n|z-x|\\ge\\kappa(|x|+|z|),\n\\qquad \\kappa=\\frac{c}{c+2}&gt;0. \\tag{34}\n\\]\nSince \\(d_2&lt;0\\), the norm-functional gap is at least \\(\\gamma\\kappa(|x|+|z|)\\). Bound \\(|a(w)|\\le\\varepsilon|w|+C_\\varepsilon\\). The fixed-metric uniform estimate in \\(U,V_n\\), with all errors at both \\(x\\) and \\(z\\), gives for sufficiently far \\(x\\), uniformly over the cut,\n\\[\n\\min\\{T_{V_n}(x,z),T_U(x,z)\\}-B_p^U(x,z)\n\\ge\\frac{\\gamma\\kappa}{2}(|x|+|z|), \\tag{35}\n\\]\nafter choosing \\(\\varepsilon&lt;\\gamma\\kappa/8\\).</p><p>For each such \\(x\\), its calibration defect tends to zero. The last-cut comparison gives eventual equality of the distances to \\(p_j\\). Distances from \\(p_0\\) along the preserved ray agree for every \\(j\\), so\n\\[\nB_p^{V_n}(p_0,x)=B_p^U(p_0,x)\n\\quad\\text{for all sufficiently far }x\\text{ with }x_2\\ge c|x|. \\tag{36}\n\\]\nTake \\(c=1/2\\), then \\(x=ke_2\\) and \\(x=k(e_1+e_2)\\). Asymptotic linearity identifies the two functionals on independent vectors, so they agree exactly. For finitely many rays, take the maximum of their thresholds. This proves assertion 1.</p><p>For assertion 2, fix \\(n\\) and an arbitrary lower \\(U\\)-ray \\(q\\) of label \\(\\lambda\\in-I\\). Put\n\\[\na_-(w)=B_q^U(0,w)-\\lambda(w)=o(|w|),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gamma_-=\\min_{|d|=1,\\ d_2\\ge0}(m(d)-\\lambda(d))&gt;0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(c\\in(0,1/2]\\) such that eventually \\(q_{j,2}\\le-c|q_j|\\). For \\(x_2\\le-c|x|\\), \\(|x|\\ge2n/c\\), and \\(z\\in H_n\\),\n\\[\n|z-x|\\ge c|x|-n\\ge\\tfrac c2|x|,\n\\qquad\n|x|+|z|\\le(1+4/c)|z-x|.\n\\]\nThe displacement \\(z-x\\) points upward. The fixed-metric estimates in \\(U,W_n\\), including errors at both endpoints, give, for sufficiently far \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\min\\{T_{W_n}(x,z),T_U(x,z)\\}-B_q^U(x,z)\n\\ge\\frac{\\gamma_-c}{2(c+4)}(|x|+|z|), \\tag{37}\n\\]\nuniformly on the cut.</p><p>Choose \\(a=q_L\\) sufficiently far along \\(q\\) that this holds and the remaining tail stays strictly below the cut. For a walk from \\(a\\) to later \\(q_j\\) touching the cut, its suffix after the last cut visit uses only edges outside \\(\\mathcal E_n^+\\), shared by \\(U,W_n\\). Horizontal cut edges are part of the prefix. The last-cut comparison makes the walk more expensive than the unchanged segment of \\(q\\). Cut-avoiding walks retain \\(U\\)-costs. Thus the tail from \\(a\\) is a \\(W_n\\)-ray, and its distances from \\(a\\) are unchanged.</p><p>For each sufficiently far \\(x\\) in the strict lower sector, its calibration defect tends to zero. The same comparison gives eventual equality of distances from \\(x\\), hence equality of tail Busemann values at \\((a,x)\\). Taking \\(x=-ke_2\\) and \\(x=-k(e_1+e_2)\\) identifies the exact label as \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>All \\(W_n\\) were included in the initial common event, so this applies to every fixed \\(n\\) and every lower ray, with its own tail index. No convergence rate for \\(W_n\\) and no uniform threshold over labels is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “Comparison after a finite increase.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), horizontal parameter \\(r\\), and cone slope \\(s\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), horizontal parameter \\(r\\), and cone slope \\(s\\). For deterministic labels \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in I\\) with \\(r(\\alpha)&lt;r(\\beta)\\), define\n\\[\n(\\alpha,\\beta)_I=\\{\\rho\\in I:r(\\alpha)&lt;r(\\rho)&lt;r(\\beta)\\}.\n\\]\nIn a base environment write \\(B_\\alpha,B_{-\\beta}\\) for the common fixed-label Busemann functions.</p><p>Let \\(T^k\\) be obtained from \\(T\\) by increasing finitely many edge weights. Assume both environments have the structural properties established above. Let \\(k,y\\) lie on one horizontal line with \\(k_1&lt;y_1\\), and suppose:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every changed edge has both endpoints in \\(\\{z:|z-k|_\\infty\\le R\\}\\), where \\(R\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In \\(T^k\\), an oriented bigeodesic through \\(k\\) has upper label \\(\\rho\\in(\\alpha,\\beta)_I\\), with arms\n   \\[\n   p^+\\subset Q^+(k,H_p),\n   \\qquad p^-\\subset Q^-(k,H_p).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Finite initial portions of the two arms have all vertices in that ball, and their total increase is at least \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The base rays from \\(y\\) of labels \\(\\alpha,-\\beta\\) lie respectively in\n   \\[\n   Q^+(y,H_c),\\qquad Q^-(y,H_c).\n   \\]</p><p>Then \\(y_1-k_1&gt;S\\) implies\n\\[\nb\\le B_\\alpha(y,k)+B_{-\\beta}(y,k), \\tag{39}\n\\]\nwhere one may take\n\\[\nS=\\max\\left\\{\nR+\\frac{R+H_c}{s},\n\\frac{H_p+H_c}{s}\n\\right\\}\n\\le C_s(1+R+H_p+H_c). \\tag{40}\n\\]\nThe separation is uniform over bins and independent of the magnitudes of individual increases.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(d=y_1-k_1\\). A vertex \\(w\\) in the radius-\\(R\\) ball satisfies\n\\[\n|w_2-y_2|\\le R,\n\\qquad |w_1-y_1|\\ge d-R.\n\\]\nMembership in either comparator cone would require \\(R\\ge s(d-R)-H_c\\). Thus\n\\[\nd&gt;R+\\frac{R+H_c}{s} \\tag{41}\n\\]\nmakes both base comparator rays avoid the ball. Their weights are unchanged in \\(T^k\\), so Proposition 1 preserves their geodesic property and labels.</p><p>The remaining separation in (40) permits Proposition 5. The upper marked ray starts left of \\(y\\) but has label strictly east of \\(\\alpha\\). The lower marked ray also has reverse label order, since\n\\[\nr(-\\beta)=-r(\\beta)&lt;-r(\\rho)=r(-\\rho).\n\\]\nTherefore the upper comparator meets \\(p^+\\), and the lower comparator meets \\(p^-\\), at vertices \\(w^+,w^-\\). By (41) these intersections are outside the ball, hence after the prescribed gain portions. This remains so if an arm leaves and reenters the ball, because all vertices of its prescribed initial portion lie in the ball.</p><p>Let \\(g^+,g^-\\) be the increases on the prescribed portions, with \\(g^++g^-\\ge b\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cost}_{T^k}(p^+[k,w^+])\n\\ge g^++\\operatorname{cost}_T(p^+[k,w^+])\n\\ge g^++B_\\alpha(k,w^+),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cost}_{T^k}(p^-[k,w^-])\n\\ge g^-+\\operatorname{cost}_T(p^-[k,w^-])\n\\ge g^-+B_{-\\beta}(k,w^-).\n\\]</p><p>The marked segment from \\(w^-\\) through \\(k\\) to \\(w^+\\) minimizes in \\(T^k\\). A competitor follows the lower comparator backward from \\(w^-\\) to \\(y\\), then the upper comparator to \\(w^+\\). Its cost is unchanged and equals\n\\[\nB_{-\\beta}(y,w^-)+B_\\alpha(y,w^+).\n\\]\nNo disjointness of these portions is needed; a finite walk is admissible and counts every traversal. Thus\n\\[\nb+B_\\alpha(k,w^+)+B_{-\\beta}(k,w^-)\n\\le B_\\alpha(y,w^+)+B_{-\\beta}(y,w^-).\n\\]\nAdditivity cancels the terminal increments and gives (39).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.2, “The label budget.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), deterministic \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in I\\) with \\(r(\\alpha)<r(\\beta)\\), integer spacing \\(l\\ge1\\), and gain \\(b>0\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), deterministic \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in I\\) with \\(r(\\alpha)&lt;r(\\beta)\\), integer spacing \\(l\\ge1\\), and gain \\(b&gt;0\\). On a common probability space mark some sites\n\\[\nk_j=(jl,0),\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nin the bin \\((\\alpha,\\beta)_I\\).</p><p>Assume:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. every grid site has the same marginal marking probability \\(q_{\\alpha,\\beta}\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. the base environment has the fixed-label Busemann asymptotics for \\(\\alpha,-\\beta\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. for successive marks \\(k&lt;y\\) in this bin, comparison (39) holds.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\frac bl\\,q_{\\alpha,\\beta}\\le r(\\beta)-r(\\alpha). \\tag{42}\n\\]\nNo independence or ergodicity of the marks is needed.</p><p>For subsequent applications, choose deterministic nested finite sets\n\\[\nD_1\\subset D_2\\subset\\cdots\\subset I\n\\]\ncontaining both chart endpoints, with union \\(D_I\\) dense in \\(I\\), and with \\(r(D_I)\\) containing every endpoint of a complementary interval of \\(r(I)\\) in its convex hull. The bins \\(\\Pi_j\\) are the open intervals between consecutive members of \\(D_j\\) in \\(r\\)-order. Their widths sum to\n\\[\nW_I=r(\\rho_r)-r(\\rho_\\ell). \\tag{38}\n\\]\nEvery two distinct labels of \\(I\\setminus D_I\\) eventually lie in different bins. Almost surely no bigeodesic label belongs to \\(D_I\\cup(-D_I)\\), in the base environment or any specified countable collection of absolutely continuous environments.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nw=r(\\beta)-r(\\alpha)&gt;0,\n\\]\nand, for \\(j\\ge0\\), write\n\\[\nA_j=B_\\alpha(0,k_j)+B_{-\\beta}(0,k_j)\n=-wjl+e_j.\n\\]\nThe sign uses\n\\[\n\\alpha(e_1)+(-\\beta)(e_1)=r(\\alpha)-r(\\beta)=-w.\n\\]\nThe fixed-label asymptotics give \\(e_j=o(jl)\\). Let\n\\[\nE_N=\\max_{0\\le j\\le N}|e_j|.\n\\]\nThen \\(E_N/(Nl)\\to0\\) almost surely: for \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), choose \\(J\\) with \\(|e_j|\\le\\delta jl\\) for \\(j\\ge J\\), and bound the finitely many earlier errors by \\(C_J\\). Thus\n\\[\nE_N\\le\\max\\{C_J,\\delta Nl\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(m_N\\) be the number of marks among \\(k_0,\\ldots,k_N\\). If \\(m_N\\ge2\\), list their indices as \\(i_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;i_{m_N}\\). Summing (39) over consecutive marks gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(m_N-1)\n&amp;\\le A_{i_1}-A_{i_{m_N}}\\\\\n&amp;=wl(i_{m_N}-i_1)+e_{i_1}-e_{i_{m_N}}\n\\le wNl+2E_N.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same bound applies to \\(b(m_N-1)_+\\) when \\(m_N\\le1\\). Hence\n\\[\nbm_N\\le b+wNl+2E_N,\n\\qquad\n\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{m_N}{Nl}\\le\\frac wb\n\\quad\\text{almost surely}. \\tag{43}\n\\]\nBecause\n\\[\n0\\le\\frac{m_N}{Nl}\\le\\frac{N+1}{Nl}\\le\\frac2l,\n\\]\nbounded reverse Fatou gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\mathbb E\\frac{m_N}{Nl}\n\\le\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{m_N}{Nl}\\right]\n\\le\\frac wb.\n\\]\nEqual marginals imply \\(\\mathbb Em_N=(N+1)q_{\\alpha,\\beta}\\). Therefore \\(q_{\\alpha,\\beta}/l\\le w/b\\). No Busemann error or increment is integrated.</p><p>For the partition assertions, the complementary intervals are countable, so their endpoints can be included in \\(D_I\\). If two distinct labels have another label between them, density eventually supplies an intervening partition point. Otherwise they are endpoints of a complementary interval and already belong to \\(D_I\\). Their exclusion as bigeodesic labels follows from Proposition 3 and absolute continuity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Usable edges on long simple paths.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the nonatomic iid edge law, there are \\(a_0<b_0\\) such that \\( p_a=\\mathbb P(t_e\\le a_0)>0, \\qquad q=\\mathbb P(t_e<b_0)<\\frac1{64}, \\qquad p_b=\\mathbb P(t_e\\ge b_0)=1-q>0. \\) Call an edge usable if \\(t_e\\ge b_0\\) …", "statement": "<p>Under the nonatomic iid edge law, there are \\(a_0&lt;b_0\\) such that\n\\[\np_a=\\mathbb P(t_e\\le a_0)&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nq=\\mathbb P(t_e&lt;b_0)&lt;\\frac1{64},\n\\qquad\np_b=\\mathbb P(t_e\\ge b_0)=1-q&gt;0. \\tag{44}\n\\]\nCall an edge usable if \\(t_e\\ge b_0\\), and put \\(\\theta=8\\sqrt q&lt;1\\).</p><p>For every \\(x\\) and integer \\(n_0\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\!\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\text{some simple path from \\(x\\) of length \\(j\\ge n_0\\)}\\\\\n\\text{has fewer than \\(j/2\\) usable edges}\n\\end{array}\n\\right)\n\\le\\frac{\\theta^{n_0}}{1-\\theta}. \\tag{45}\n\\]\nIf \\(U_n\\) is the event that every simple path starting in\n\\[\n[-3n,3n]^2\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\n\\]\nand having length at least \\(n/2\\) has at least half its edges usable, then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(U_n^c)\n\\le(6n+1)^2\\frac{\\theta^{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}}{1-\\theta}\n\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{46}\n\\]\nAlmost surely, at every vertex all sufficiently long simple paths satisfy this fraction bound. No moment of an individual edge weight is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(a\\) be the bottom of the support of \\(G\\). It is finite, and \\(G(\\{a\\})=0\\). As \\(b\\downarrow a\\), \\(b&gt;a\\), the probability \\(G([0,b))\\) tends to zero and is positive. Choose \\(b_0&gt;a\\) with this probability below \\(1/64\\), and choose \\(a_0\\in(a,b_0)\\). The definition of \\(a\\) gives \\(p_a&gt;0\\).</p><p>A simple path has distinct edges. For a specified path of length \\(j\\), independence and union bound over subsets bound the probability that at least \\(j/2\\) edges are unusable by\n\\[\n2^jq^{j/2}.\n\\]\nThis also bounds the probability of fewer than \\(j/2\\) usable edges. There are at most \\(4^j\\) nearest-neighbor paths of length \\(j\\) from a fixed vertex, so the union bound over simple paths is at most \\(\\theta^j\\). Summation over \\(j\\ge n_0\\) proves (45). Union over the \\((6n+1)^2\\) possible starting vertices proves (46).</p><p>The events in (45) decrease as \\(n_0\\to\\infty\\), and their probabilities tend to zero. Countability gives the final assertion simultaneously at all vertices.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 5.4, “Expected label count at an edge.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), its slope \\(s\\), partitions from Proposition 9, and thresholds \\(a_0,b_0\\) from Proposition 10.", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), its slope \\(s\\), partitions from Proposition 9, and thresholds \\(a_0,b_0\\) from Proposition 10.</p><p>For an edge \\(e=\\{x,x+v\\}\\), where \\(v\\) is a lattice unit vector, and \\(H\\ge1\\), let \\(\\mathcal R_e(H)\\) be the event that, from each endpoint \\(z\\) of \\(e\\), every upper-label ray lies in \\(Q^+(z,H)\\), and every lower-label ray lies in \\(Q^-(z,H)\\).</p><p>Set \\(N_e(H)=0\\) if \\(e\\) is unusable or \\(\\mathcal R_e(H)\\) fails. Otherwise define\n\\[\nN_e(H)\n=\\#\\{\\rho\\in I:e\\text{ lies on a bigeodesic of upper label }\\rho\\}. \\tag{47}\n\\]\nThis counts distinct labels and is \\(+\\infty\\) if infinitely many occur. It is measurable in the completed probability space; the universal regularity event is imposed as a separate environment-only filter.</p><p>There is \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on the chart and law, such that for every lattice edge \\(e\\) and \\(H\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb EN_e(H)\\le C(H+1). \\tag{48}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(v\\), and let \\(e_k=\\{k,k+v\\}\\) for \\(k\\) on a horizontal grid. Use one iid base environment \\(t\\) and independent samples \\(\\widehat t_{e_k}\\) with law \\(G\\). Let \\(t^k\\) equal \\(t\\) off \\(e_k\\) and \\(\\widehat t_{e_k}\\) on \\(e_k\\). Each individual environment is iid. Take the common full-measure event for the base and these countably many environments, including all fixed partition-endpoint exclusions.</p><p>For a bin \\((\\alpha,\\beta)_I\\), let \\(A_e^{\\alpha,\\beta}(H)\\) be the event that \\(e\\) is usable, \\(\\mathcal R_e(H)\\) holds, and a bigeodesic with upper label in the bin uses \\(e\\). Mark \\(k\\) when\n\\[\nt_{e_k}\\le a_0\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nA_{e_k}^{\\alpha,\\beta}(H)\\text{ holds in }t^k. \\tag{49}\n\\]\nThe original \\(t_{e_k}\\) is independent of the entire \\(t^k\\), so\n\\[\nq_{\\alpha,\\beta}\n=p_a\\mathbb P(A_e^{\\alpha,\\beta}(H)). \\tag{50}\n\\]\nThe regularity test is measurable in the individual output and does not affect this calculation.</p><p>On a mark, \\(e_k\\) is increased by at least\n\\[\nb=b_0-a_0&gt;0.\n\\]\nA witnessing bigeodesic passes through \\(k\\), and \\(e_k\\) is the first edge of one arm. Use it as that arm’s gain portion and use the empty portion of the other arm. Both the modification and gain portions lie in a radius-one ball. The marked arms have cone offset \\(H\\).</p><p>We also need base comparator control at every marked site. A simple base ray from \\(k\\) can traverse \\(e_k\\) only as its first edge, since any later traversal revisits \\(k\\). If it avoids \\(e_k\\), monotonicity preserves it in \\(t^k\\), and Proposition 1 preserves its label. Therefore it lies in its \\(t^k\\)-cone at \\(k\\).</p><p>If it uses \\(e_k\\) first, its tail from \\(k+v\\) avoids the changed edge and remains a ray with the same label. Regularity at \\(k+v\\) gives\n\\[\n\\pm(w_2-k_2-v_2)\\ge s|w_1-k_1-v_1|-H,\n\\]\nwhich implies\n\\[\n\\pm(w_2-k_2)\n\\ge s|w_1-k_1|-(H+|v_2|+s|v_1|).\n\\]\nIncluding \\(k\\), and the initial edge by convexity, confines the base ray with offset at most \\(H+1+s\\). This controls the base comparators \\(\\alpha,-\\beta\\), uniformly over bins.</p><p>Choose integer spacing \\(l(H)\\) large enough for Proposition 8 with\n\\[\nR=1,\\qquad H_p=H,\\qquad H_c=H+1+s,\n\\]\nand so that\n\\[\n2\\le l(H)\\le C_0(H+1). \\tag{51}\n\\]\nThe lower bound makes the grid edges distinct. Any two marks are sufficiently separated. Propositions 8 and 9, together with (50), yield\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(A_e^{\\alpha,\\beta}(H))\n\\le\\frac{l(H)}{(b_0-a_0)p_a}\\bigl(r(\\beta)-r(\\alpha)\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(K_j(e,H)\\) count the occupied bins of \\(\\Pi_j\\) among labels counted by \\(N_e(H)\\). Summing the last inequality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EK_j(e,H)\n\\le\\frac{l(H)W_I}{(b_0-a_0)p_a}. \\tag{52}\n\\]\nOn the endpoint-exclusion event, refinement loses no label, so \\(K_j(e,H)\\) is nondecreasing. Every finite collection of distinct counted labels is eventually in distinct bins. Thus\n\\[\nK_j(e,H)\\uparrow N_e(H),\n\\]\nalso if the latter is infinite. Monotone convergence and (51) prove (48). Translation invariance and finitely many edge directions give one constant for all \\(e\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, “Absence of forks.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\). An upper fork at \\(x\\) consists of two bigeodesics, oriented toward upper labels in \\(I\\), with the same upper label, coincident backward from \\(x\\), and with different first forward edges from \\(x\\). Equivalently, they have a common backward tail and split at \\(x\\). A lower fork is defined by reversing orientation.</p><p>These are completed-measurable existence events with two path witnesses and one label witness.</p><p>Almost surely there is no upper or lower fork in any of the countably many charts.</p>", "proof": "<p>First exclude upper forks in one chart. Work on the simultaneous event of Proposition 7. Every upper fork label is exceptional by branching, so all lower rays of its opposite label coalesce, simultaneously for every such label. A finite collection of bigeodesics with one such upper label therefore has a common backward tail and a common past root sufficiently far along it.</p><p>Let \\(F_n\\) count upper-fork vertices in \\([-n,n]^2\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), and put\n\\[\np_{\\rm fork}=\\mathbb P(0\\text{ is an upper fork in this chart}).\n\\]\nTranslation invariance gives\n\\[\n0\\le F_n\\le(2n+1)^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb EF_n=(2n+1)^2p_{\\rm fork}. \\tag{53}\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), let \\(H_n=\\lceil\\varepsilon n\\rceil\\), and let \\(G_n\\) be the intersection of \\(U_n\\) from Proposition 10 with the event that \\(\\mathcal R_e(H_n)\\) holds for every edge having both endpoints in \\([-2n,2n]^2\\). Proposition 4 gives the latter event eventually almost surely, because all required offsets in this box are \\(o(n)\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(G_n^c)\\longrightarrow0 \\tag{54}\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>On \\(G_n\\), for \\(n\\ge2\\), we claim\n\\[\n\\frac n4F_n\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\substack{e:\\text{both endpoints}\\\\\\text{in }[-2n,2n]^2}}N_e(H_n). \\tag{55}\n\\]\nChoose a witnessing pair at each counted fork and group the finite collection by exact upper label. These choices establish a pathwise inequality and need no measurable enumeration.</p><p>Consider a group of \\(q\\ge1\\) fork sites, with at most \\(2q\\) witnessing bigeodesics. Their lower rays coalesce by Proposition 7. Choose a common-tail vertex \\(o\\) earlier than all selected splits and orient each witness forward from \\(o\\).</p><p>Their union is a rooted tree. If witnesses visit a common vertex \\(z\\), their paths from \\(o\\) to \\(z\\) are identical by finite uniqueness. They cannot diverge and reunite; common edges cannot have conflicting forward orientations; each vertex other than \\(o\\) has one parent.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the finite subtree consisting of root paths to the two selected children immediately following every chosen split. Each of the \\(q\\) split vertices has at least two children. For a finite rooted tree with \\(L\\) leaves,\n\\[\nL-1=\\sum_{u:d_{\\mathcal T}^+(u)&gt;0}(d_{\\mathcal T}^+(u)-1). \\tag{56}\n\\]\nIndeed, the sum of outdegrees is the number of edges, one less than the number of vertices; subtract the number of nonleaves. Thus \\(L\\ge q+1\\).</p><p>Every leaf is the endpoint of one of the defining root-to-child paths; otherwise that path continues inside \\(\\mathcal T\\), contradicting leafhood. Hence every leaf is a selected child of a fork site and lies in \\([-n-1,n+1]^2\\). Root paths may leave and reenter the boxes; this does not alter the leaf locations. Selected split vertices may be ancestors of one another but are distinct branching vertices in (56).</p><p>Continue each leaf forward on a witness containing its defining path. The continuations are pairwise vertex disjoint. If two met, finite uniqueness from \\(o\\) to the meeting vertex would put both leaves on one root path, making them comparable, contrary to distinct leafhood. The same reasoning excludes contact with another leaf’s root path. Contact with an earlier point on its own root path violates simplicity. These conclusions hold throughout the plane.</p><p>Each leaf has infinity-norm distance at least \\(n-1\\) from the boundary of \\([-2n,2n]^2\\). Its upper continuation eventually reaches this boundary. Stop at the first visit. The stopped path is simple, lies in the outer box, and has length at least \\(n-1\\ge n/2\\). Its start lies in \\([-3n,3n]^2\\), so \\(U_n\\) gives at least \\(n/4\\) usable edges. On \\(G_n\\), each such edge satisfies \\(\\mathcal R_e(H_n)\\) and contributes this group’s label to \\(N_e(H_n)\\).</p><p>Within a group, disjoint continuations prevent duplicate credits for the same edge and label. Different groups credit different edge-label pairs. Summing gives at least\n\\[\n\\frac n4\\sum_{\\rm groups}L\n\\ge\\frac n4\\sum_{\\rm groups}q\n=\\frac n4F_n,\n\\]\nproving (55).</p><p>By (53),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[F_n\\mathbf1_{G_n}]\n\\ge(2n+1)^2\\bigl(p_{\\rm fork}-\\mathbb P(G_n^c)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe bad-event loss after multiplying by \\(n/4\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac n4(2n+1)^2\\mathbb P(G_n^c)=o(n^3) \\tag{57}\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\). There are\n\\[\n2(4n)(4n+1)\\le40n^2\n\\]\nedges in the outer box. Proposition 11 and (55) give\n\\[\n\\frac n4(2n+1)^2\n\\bigl(p_{\\rm fork}-\\mathbb P(G_n^c)\\bigr)\n\\le40Cn^2(\\lceil\\varepsilon n\\rceil+1). \\tag{58}\n\\]\nDivide by \\(n^3\\) and let \\(n\\to\\infty\\). Then \\(p_{\\rm fork}\\le40C\\varepsilon\\). Send \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), obtaining \\(p_{\\rm fork}=0\\).</p><p>Countability excludes every upper fork in this chart. Reverse upper and lower, then take the countable union over charts.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Corollary 6.2, “Common Busemann pairs for a common label.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On one probability-one event, whenever two oriented bigeodesics \\(p,q\\) in a fixed chart have the same upper label, their end Busemann functions satisfy \\( B_{p^+}=B_{q^+}, \\qquad B_{p^-}=B_{q^-}. \\) This is …", "statement": "<p>On one probability-one event, whenever two oriented bigeodesics \\(p,q\\) in a fixed chart have the same upper label, their end Busemann functions satisfy\n\\[\nB_{p^+}=B_{q^+},\n\\qquad\nB_{p^-}=B_{q^-}. \\tag{59}\n\\]\nThis is simultaneous over all such bigeodesics and their possibly environment-dependent labels.</p><p>The assertion holds almost surely in every environment whose law is absolutely continuous with respect to the iid law, and simultaneously for any countable collection of such environments.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the common full-measure event of Propositions 7 and 12 and the structural properties. Let \\(p,q\\) have upper label \\(\\rho\\).</p><p>If their upper Busemann functions differ, \\(\\rho\\) is exceptional by the second test in Proposition 6. Proposition 7 makes their lower rays coalesce. Choose \\(o\\) sufficiently far along the common lower tail. The forward portions from \\(o\\) are upper geodesic rays. They cannot have the same tail, since tail invariance would make their upper Busemann functions equal. They have a first split, and finite uniqueness prevents reunion. This is an upper fork with common past, contradicting Proposition 12.</p><p>If their lower Busemann functions differ, \\(-\\rho\\) is exceptional in the lower sector. Proposition 7 with sectors reversed coalesces the upper rays. Reversing the preceding argument gives a forbidden lower fork.</p><p>These deductions are pathwise on one event. They neither intersect uncountably many fixed-label events nor assert coalescence of every one-sided family at random labels. Absolute continuity and countable intersection give the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 7.4, “Marks with an arbitrary toll,” together with the coupling of Section 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a chart \\(I\\), its slope \\(s\\), and partitions \\(\\Pi_j\\) from Proposition 9.", "statement": "<p>Fix a chart \\(I\\), its slope \\(s\\), and partitions \\(\\Pi_j\\) from Proposition 9. Assume that, for some deterministic \\(H_0\\ge1\\), the iid event \\(\\mathcal B(0)\\) has probability \\(p&gt;0\\), where \\(\\mathcal B(0)\\) asserts existence of an oriented bigeodesic through \\(0\\), with upper label in \\(I\\setminus D_I\\) and arms in \\(Q^\\pm(0,H_0)\\). Write \\(\\mathcal B(k)\\) for its horizontal translate to \\(k=(k_1,0)\\).</p><p>Use thresholds from Proposition 10, and set\n\\[\np_a=G([0,a_0])&gt;0,\\qquad\np_b=G([b_0,\\infty))&gt;0,\\qquad\ng=b_0-a_0&gt;0.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mu=G^{\\mathcal E}\\). Independently for each edge take mutually independent\n\\[\nX_e\\sim G,\\qquad U_e\\sim{\\rm Unif}[0,1],\n\\qquad Z_e\\sim G(\\,\\cdot\\mid[b_0,\\infty)).\n\\]\nThe base environment is \\(X\\). Put\n\\[\nh(e)=\\max\\{|w_2|,|w'_2|\\}\n\\quad\\text{for }e=\\{w,w'\\},\n\\qquad\n\\delta_h=\\frac{\\eta}{(h+2)\\log(h+2)},\n\\]\nwhere one sufficiently small \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) is fixed independently of the requested gain. Require \\(\\delta_0\\le p_a/2\\), and define\n\\[\n\\xi_e=\\mathbf1_{\\{X_e\\le a_0,\\ U_e\\le\\delta_{h(e)}/p_a\\}},\n\\qquad\n\\widehat X_e=\n\\begin{cases}\nZ_e,&amp;\\xi_e=1,\\\\\nX_e,&amp;\\xi_e=0.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{60}\n\\]\nThen \\(\\widehat X_e\\ge X_e\\), and a successful raise increases cost by at least \\(g\\).</p><p>For horizontal \\(k\\) and integer \\(R\\ge1\\), let\n\\[\nA_k(R)=\n\\left\\{\ne:h(e)\\le R,\\\n\\max_{w\\text{ endpoint of }e}|w_1-k_1|\n\\le1+\\frac{h(e)+H_0}{s}\n\\right\\}. \\tag{61}\n\\]\nLet \\(Y^{k,R}\\) use \\(\\widehat X_e\\) on \\(A_k(R)\\) and \\(X_e\\) elsewhere, with metric \\(T^{k,R}\\). Let \\(Y^{k,y,R}\\) use \\(\\widehat X_e\\) on \\(A_k(R)\\cup A_y(R)\\), with metric \\(T^{k,y,R}\\). Overlaps use the same sample once.</p><p>Every individual and union environment has law absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\mu\\). There is a fixed integer \\(R_0\\) such that, for every positive integer \\(M\\), a deterministic \\(R(M)\\ge R_0\\) and marks \\(J_M(k)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) can be constructed with equal horizontal marginals and\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(J_M(k)=1)\\ge p/4. \\tag{73}\n\\]\nA mark selects a bigeodesic \\(p_k\\) in \\(T^{k,R(M)}\\), through \\(k\\), with upper label \\(\\rho_k\\in I\\setminus D_I\\), arms in \\(Q^\\pm(k,H_0)\\), total increase at least \\(M\\) on its first \\(R(M)\\) upper-arm edges, and an increase at least \\(g\\) on one of its first \\(R_0\\) upper-arm edges.</p><p>All changes lie in a deterministic ball about \\(k\\) of radius at most\n\\[\nD_R=R+1+\\frac{R+H_0}{s}. \\tag{74}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 14.1 proves absolute continuity and permits the fixed choice of \\(\\eta\\) in Lemma 14.3. Proposition 10 supplies its \\(n_0\\).</p><p>The total variation bound and the iid failure bound give, uniformly in \\(k,R\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\mathcal E_{k,R})\n\\ge p-p/8-p/8\\ge p/2. \\tag{69}\n\\]\nProposition 1 permits selection of a witness and label from the individual output alone. Select in the completed iid space on \\(\\mathcal B(0)\\), replace by a Borel version, and translate this rule. Absolute continuity validates it in every countable individual output. The usable-prefix condition is already determined by the output and adds no information to Lemma 14.2.</p><p>Take \\(R=R(M)\\) from Lemma 14.3. Mark exactly when \\(\\mathcal E_{k,R}\\) holds and both success tests hold. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(J_M(k)=1)\\ge(p/2)(3/4)=3p/8\\ge p/4.\n\\]\nAll selected path properties follow from selection and the marking tests. Formula (61) puts \\(A_k(R)\\) in the ball of radius (74); the gain prefix lies in the radius-\\(R\\) ball.</p><p>Horizontal translations preserve the triples’ distribution and height indices. Since the selector and tests are translated in the same way, marks have equal marginals; in fact the construction is horizontally stationary. No independence between marks is asserted.</p><p>The conditional product law has now served its purpose. Later filters may depend on the base environment or other marks and bias the hidden raises. Only the unconditional lower bound (73) will subsequently be used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, “Uniform change of law.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the coupling in Proposition 14, every \\(Y^{k,R}\\) and \\(Y^{k,y,R}\\) has law absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\mu\\).", "statement": "<p>For the coupling in Proposition 14, every \\(Y^{k,R}\\) and \\(Y^{k,y,R}\\) has law absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\mu\\). There is \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on \\(s,H_0,p_a,p_b\\), such that\n\\[\nL_{k,R}=\\frac{d\\mathcal L(Y^{k,R})}{d\\mu}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\mu L_{k,R}^2\\le e^{C\\eta^2},\n\\qquad\n\\|\\mathcal L(Y^{k,R})-\\mu\\|_{\\rm TV}\n\\le\\frac12\\sqrt{e^{C\\eta^2}-1}. \\tag{63}\n\\]\nThe bounds are uniform in \\(k,R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(h(e)=h\\), the law of \\(\\widehat X_e\\) has density relative to \\(G\\)\n\\[\n\\lambda_h(t)\n=1-\\frac{\\delta_h}{p_a}\\mathbf1_{[0,a_0]}(t)\n+\\frac{\\delta_h}{p_b}\\mathbf1_{[b_0,\\infty)}(t). \\tag{64}\n\\]\nThe indicated sets are disjoint. Direct integration gives \\(\\int\\lambda_h\\,dG=1\\) and\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\lambda_h^2\\,dG\n&amp;=p_a(1-\\delta_h/p_a)^2\n+p_b(1+\\delta_h/p_b)^2+1-p_a-p_b\\\\\n&amp;=1+(p_a^{-1}+p_b^{-1})\\delta_h^2. \n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(65)\n\\]\nOn any deterministic finite replaced set, independence gives the product density; the exterior retains its iid law. This proves absolute continuity for both individual and union environments.</p><p>Let \\(N_h\\) count edges of height \\(h\\) in \\(A_k(R)\\). At positive height \\(h\\), horizontal edges lie in the two rows \\(h,-h\\), and vertical edges in the two adjacent strips toward height zero. Each row or strip has at most\n\\[\n2(1+(h+H_0)/s)+1\n\\]\nadmissible edges. At height zero only one horizontal row occurs. Thus one may use\n\\[\nN_h\\le c_s(h+H_0+1),\\qquad c_s=12+8/s.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_\\mu L_{k,R}^2\n&amp;=\\prod_{e\\in A_k(R)}\n\\left[1+(p_a^{-1}+p_b^{-1})\\delta_{h(e)}^2\\right]\\\\\n&amp;\\le\\exp\\left\\{\nc_s(p_a^{-1}+p_b^{-1})\\eta^2\n\\sum_{h=0}^\\infty\n\\frac{h+H_0+1}{(h+2)^2\\log^2(h+2)}\n\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe series is finite; leading terms have order \\(1/(h\\log^2h)\\). It defines \\(C\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\mathbb E_\\mu L_{k,R}=1\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal L(Y^{k,R})-\\mu\\|_{\\rm TV}\n=\\tfrac12\\mathbb E_\\mu|L_{k,R}-1|\n\\le\\tfrac12\\sqrt{\\mathbb E_\\mu L_{k,R}^2-1}.\n\\]\nOnly finite modified sets are used; the infinite series uniformly bounds their finite products.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Conditioning on an individual output.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(k,R\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(k,R\\). Conditional on the entire individual environment \\(Y^{k,R}\\), the variables \\((\\xi_e:e\\in A_k(R))\\) are independent Bernoulli variables with success probabilities\n\\[\n\\theta_e(Y_e^{k,R})\n=\\mathbf1_{[b_0,\\infty)}(Y_e^{k,R})\n\\frac{\\delta_{h(e)}}{p_b+\\delta_{h(e)}}. \\tag{66}\n\\]\nThis includes conditioning on every unchanged exterior edge.</p><p>If a simple path is selected measurably from \\(Y^{k,R}\\) alone, the same product law holds on each finite prefix lying in \\(A_k(R)\\), once conditioning fixes that prefix. No other site’s output is included in the conditioning.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(e\\in A_k(R)\\), put \\(Y_e=\\widehat X_e\\) and \\(h=h(e)\\). As measures in \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\xi_e=1,Y_e\\in dt)\n=\\frac{\\delta_h}{p_b}\\mathbf1_{[b_0,\\infty)}(t)G(dt), \\tag{67}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(\\xi_e=0,Y_e\\in dt)\n=\\left[1-\\frac{\\delta_h}{p_a}\\mathbf1_{[0,a_0]}(t)\\right]G(dt). \\tag{68}\n\\]\nFor (67), multiply the low-input probability \\(p_a\\), replacement probability \\(\\delta_h/p_a\\), and conditional law of \\(Z_e\\). Equation (68) retains the original sample except for removed low-set mass. Dividing (67) by the sum density (64) gives (66).</p><p>The pairs \\((\\xi_e,Y_e)\\) are independent across \\(A_k(R)\\), so their conditional kernel given the finite output vector is the product of these one-edge kernels. Exterior outputs are original \\(X_e\\)’s independent of the inside triples. Testing against bounded functions of finitely many exterior coordinates preserves the conditional identity; a monotone-class argument extends it to all exterior coordinates.</p><p>A completion-measurable selector has a Borel version under the output law. Conditional on the output, a finite prefix of this version is a fixed list of distinct edges. Restrict the product kernel to that list. Equivalently, partition by the countably many possible prefixes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, “Divergent gain along selected prefixes.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(\\eta>0\\) so that \\(\\delta_0\\le p_a/2\\) and the total variation bound in Lemma 14.1 is at most \\(p/8\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) so that \\(\\delta_0\\le p_a/2\\) and the total variation bound in Lemma 14.1 is at most \\(p/8\\).</p><p>Choose deterministic \\(n_0\\) so large that the iid event\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_{n_0}(k)\n=\\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{c}\n\\text{every simple path from \\(k\\) of each length \\(d\\ge n_0\\)}\\\\\n\\text{has at least \\(d/2\\) usable edges}\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}\n\\]\nhas failure probability at most \\(p/8\\). Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{k,R}\n=\\{Y^{k,R}\\in\\mathcal B(k)\\cap\\mathcal U_{n_0}(k)\\}.\n\\]\nUsing \\(Y^{k,R}\\) alone, select a witnessing oriented bigeodesic on \\(\\mathcal E_{k,R}\\), with the same translated selection rule at all horizontal sites.</p><p>There is a fixed integer \\(R_0\\ge n_0\\) such that, for every positive integer \\(M\\), one can choose deterministic \\(R(M)\\ge R_0\\) with this property: on \\(\\mathcal E_{k,R(M)}\\), conditional on \\(Y^{k,R(M)}\\), with probability at least \\(3/4\\), the selected upper arm has a strict raise among its first \\(R_0\\) edges and total increase at least \\(M\\) among its first \\(R(M)\\) edges.</p>", "proof": "<p>A bigeodesic through horizontal \\(k\\) with arms in \\(Q^\\pm(k,H_0)\\) satisfies, for every vertex \\(w\\) on either arm,\n\\[\ns|w_1-k_1|\\le|w_2|+H_0. \\tag{62}\n\\]\nThus every edge of height at most \\(R\\) lies in \\(A_k(R)\\); in particular the first \\(R\\) upper-arm edges do, since the \\(j\\)-th edge has height at most \\(j\\).</p><p>Fix an output in \\(\\mathcal E_{k,R}\\), and let the selected upper-arm edges be \\(e_1,e_2,\\ldots\\). For \\(d\\le R\\), put\n\\[\na_j=\\mathbf1_{\\{Y_{e_j}^{k,R}\\ge b_0\\}},\n\\quad\nA_d=\\sum_{j=1}^da_j,\n\\quad\nN_d=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\xi_{e_j},\n\\quad\n\\mu_d=\\mathbb E(N_d\\mid Y^{k,R}).\n\\]\nThe edges and \\(a_j\\)’s are fixed by conditioning, and \\(A_d\\ge d/2\\) for \\(d\\ge n_0\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nf_j=\\frac1{(j+2)\\log(j+2)},\n\\qquad\n\\gamma=\\frac{\\eta}{p_b+\\delta_0}&gt;0.\n\\]\nLemma 14.2 and \\(h(e_j)\\le j\\) give\n\\[\n\\mu_d\\ge\\gamma\\sum_{j=1}^da_jf_j. \\tag{70}\n\\]\nSummation by parts yields, for \\(d\\ge n_0\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{j=1}^da_jf_j\n&amp;=A_df_d+\\sum_{j=1}^{d-1}A_j(f_j-f_{j+1})\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac12\\left[df_d+\\sum_{j=n_0}^{d-1}j(f_j-f_{j+1})\\right]\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac12\\left[n_0f_{n_0}+\\sum_{j=n_0+1}^df_j\\right].\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(71)\n\\]\nThe last expression tends to infinity. Let \\(m_d\\) be \\(\\gamma\\) times that expression, a deterministic increasing lower bound for \\(\\mu_d\\).</p><p>The path is simple. Lemma 14.2 gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(N_d\\mid Y^{k,R})\n=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\theta_{e_j}(1-\\theta_{e_j})\n\\le\\mu_d. \\tag{72}\n\\]\nChoose \\(R_0\\ge n_0\\) with \\(m_{R_0}\\ge8\\). For all \\(R\\ge R_0\\), Chebyshev gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(N_{R_0}=0\\mid Y^{k,R})\n\\le\\mu_{R_0}^{-1}\\le1/8.\n\\]\nThis choice is independent of \\(R,M\\).</p><p>For gain \\(M\\), put \\(K_M=\\lceil M/g\\rceil\\), and choose \\(R=R(M)\\ge R_0\\) so that\n\\[\nm_R\\ge\\max\\{2K_M,32\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(N_R&lt;K_M\\mid Y^{k,R})\n\\le\\frac{\\mu_R}{(\\mu_R-K_M)^2}\n\\le\\frac4{\\mu_R}\\le1/8.\n\\]\nAt least \\(K_M\\) successful raises give gain at least \\(gK_M\\ge M\\). Union bound for the two failures gives conditional success probability at least \\(3/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 7.6, “No repeated marked label.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use the coupling, marks, fixed \\(R_0\\), and radii \\(R(M)\\) of Proposition 14.", "statement": "<p>Use the coupling, marks, fixed \\(R_0\\), and radii \\(R(M)\\) of Proposition 14. Choose an integer spacing \\(l\\), once and for all, with\n\\[\nl&gt;R_0+1+\\frac{R_0+H_0}{s}, \\tag{75}\n\\]\nand restrict marks to\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\{(jl,0):j\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n\\]\nThe spacing is independent of \\(M\\).</p><p>Almost surely, for every positive integer \\(M\\), the selected upper labels at distinct marked sites of \\(\\Lambda\\) are distinct.</p><p>Work on the common probability-one event for the base environment and every individual and two-site union environment, for all integer radii and horizontal sites, on which Propositions 1, 3, 4, and 13 hold and partition-endpoint bigeodesic labels are excluded. Such an event exists by Proposition 14 and countable absolute continuity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the common event in the statement. Suppose distinct marked sites \\(k,y\\) have \\(\\rho_k=\\rho_y\\). Lemma 15.1 preserves both bigeodesics with that label in \\(T^*=T^{k,y,R}\\). Proposition 13 identifies their upper and lower Busemann functions there; call the common pair \\(B^+,B^-\\).</p><p>For \\(u\\in p_y\\), inequality (6), based first at \\(k\\) on \\(p_k\\) and then at \\(u\\) on \\(p_y\\), gives\n\\[\nB^+(k,u)+B^-(k,u)\\le0,\n\\qquad\nB^+(u,k)+B^-(u,k)\\le0.\n\\]\nAntisymmetry implies\n\\[\nB^+(k,u)+B^-(k,u)=0\n\\qquad(u\\in p_y). \\tag{76}\n\\]</p><p>Choose a raised edge \\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\) among the first \\(R_0\\) upper-arm edges of \\(p_y\\), oriented toward the upper end from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\). If \\(t_e^*\\) is its union weight, calibration gives\n\\[\nB^+(u,v)=T^*(u,v)=t_e^*. \\tag{77}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(x_N^-,x_N^+\\) tend to the lower and upper ends of \\(p_k\\), with \\(k\\) between them. Define\n\\[\nL_N^*=T^*(x_N^-,u)+t_e^*+T^*(v,x_N^+),\n\\qquad\nD_N^*=T^*(x_N^-,x_N^+).\n\\]\nThe first is the cost of an admissible walk, so \\(L_N^*\\ge D_N^*\\). Since \\(p_k\\) is a bigeodesic,\n\\[\nD_N^*=T^*(x_N^-,k)+T^*(k,x_N^+).\n\\]\nThe Busemann definition yields\n\\[\nT^*(x_N^-,u)-T^*(x_N^-,k)\\longrightarrow-B^-(k,u),\n\\]\n\\[\nT^*(v,x_N^+)-T^*(k,x_N^+)\\longrightarrow-B^+(k,v).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_N^*-D_N^*\n&amp;\\longrightarrow-B^-(k,u)-B^+(k,v)+t_e^*\\\\\n&amp;=-[B^-(k,u)+B^+(k,u)]-B^+(u,v)+t_e^*=0,\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(78)\n\\]\nby (76)–(77).</p><p>Choose minimizing approach paths in \\(T^*\\) from \\(x_N^-\\) to \\(u\\) and from \\(v\\) to \\(x_N^+\\), and join them by the specified traversal \\(u\\to v\\). Call the resulting finite walk \\(W_N\\). Its approach paths may intersect each other, the bigeodesics, or \\(e\\); its cost is \\(L_N^*\\), counting every traversal.</p><p>Lemma 15.1 says that \\(e\\) has base weight \\(X_e\\) in \\(T^{k,R}\\), so\n\\[\n\\Delta=t_e^*-X_e\\ge g&gt;0.\n\\]\nReturning to \\(T^{k,R}\\) weakly decreases every traversal cost, and the named traversal decreases by \\(\\Delta\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cost}_{T^{k,R}}(W_N)\\le L_N^*-\\Delta. \\tag{79}\n\\]\nFurther traversals of \\(e\\) only increase the saving. The \\(p_k\\)-segment has cost \\(D_N^*\\) in both metrics by Lemma 15.1. For sufficiently large finite \\(N\\), (78) makes \\(L_N^*-D_N^*&lt;\\Delta\\); (79) then gives a strictly cheaper walk than this geodesic segment in \\(T^{k,R}\\), a contradiction.</p><p>The argument is pathwise for every marked pair on the common event, and countability gives all positive integers \\(M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Preservation in a union.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fix \\(M\\), put \\(R=R(M)\\), and suppose distinct \\(k,y\\in\\Lambda\\) are marked.", "statement": "<p>Fix \\(M\\), put \\(R=R(M)\\), and suppose distinct \\(k,y\\in\\Lambda\\) are marked. Their selected paths \\(p_k,p_y\\) are bigeodesics in\n\\[\nT^*=T^{k,y,R}.\n\\]\nEvery edge of \\(p_k\\) has the same weight in \\(T^*\\) as in \\(T^{k,R}\\), and every edge of \\(p_y\\) has the same weight as in \\(T^{y,R}\\). Their labels are preserved.</p><p>Any raised edge among the first \\(R_0\\) upper-arm edges of \\(p_y\\) lies outside \\(A_k(R)\\), has its base weight in \\(T^{k,R}\\), and cannot lie on \\(p_k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an edge of \\(p_k\\) of height at most \\(R\\), inequality (62) puts both endpoints within the width bound of \\(A_k(R)\\). Its individual weight is already \\(\\widehat X_e\\), so passing to the union does not change it. At greater height neither region changes the base weight. This holds along all of \\(p_k\\), including excursions of either arm to the opposite side of row zero. The same applies to \\(p_y\\).</p><p>All other weights weakly increase from an individual environment to the union. Thus every segment of its selected bigeodesic remains minimizing: its cost is unchanged and no competitor becomes cheaper. The change is finite, so Proposition 1 preserves the retained end labels.</p><p>If \\(e\\) is among the first \\(R_0\\) edges of \\(p_y\\), its endpoints satisfy\n\\[\n|w_1-y_1|\\le R_0,\\qquad h(e)\\le R_0.\n\\]\nBy (75),\n\\[\n|w_1-k_1|\n\\ge|y_1-k_1|-R_0\n&gt;1+\\frac{R_0+H_0}{s}\n\\ge1+\\frac{h(e)+H_0}{s}.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(e\\notin A_k(R)\\). A raise in \\(T^{y,R}\\) increases its union weight over its \\(T^{k,R}\\)-weight by at least \\(g\\). Such an edge cannot lie on \\(p_k\\), because every \\(p_k\\)-edge in this height slab already belongs to \\(A_k(R)\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 9, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [1, 14, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [1, 19, "cite"], [1, 21, "cite"], [1, 23, "cite"], [1, 26, "cite"], [1, 29, "cite"], [1, 33, "cite"], [1, 34, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [9, 21, "cite"], [9, 24, "cite"], [9, 33, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [13, 27, "cite"], [13, 33, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 23, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [18, 28, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 27, "cite"], [20, 28, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 26, "cite"], [24, 29, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [25, 29, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 33, "cite"], [29, 0, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [30, 32, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 29, "cite"], [33, 0, "cite"], [34, 33, "cite"]]});
